},{"seed":116,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-115","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"Customer Support, Multi-Agent Research, Code Gen/CI scenarios all touched","fontSize":12.5,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"Customer Support, Multi-Agent Research, Code Gen/CI scenarios all touched","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":12.5,"originalText":"Customer Support, Multi-Agent Research, Code Gen/CI scenarios all touched","height":24,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#b4590c","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":117,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":1700,"angle":0,"y":508,"width":620},{"seed":119,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-118","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"CORE CONCEPTS — FULL DEPTH","fontSize":26,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"CORE CONCEPTS — FULL DEPTH","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":26,"originalText":"CORE CONCEPTS — FULL DEPTH","height":36,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#0f3d8a","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":120,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":0,"angle":0,"y":570,"width":2400},{"isDeleted":false,"locked":false,"strokeColor":"#2457c5","link":null,"roughness":1,"groupIds":[],"backgroundColor":"#f1f3f9","angle":0,"fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"version":1,"roundness":{"type":3},"type":"rectangle","updated":1791909281572,"id":"r-121","x":0,"seed":122,"boundElements":null,"y":618,"opacity":100,"height":441.52000000000004,"width":1150,"frameId":null,"strokeStyle":"solid","versionNonce":123},{"seed":125,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-124","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"01 · 1.1 — THE FULL stop_reason SET & LOOP GUARDRAILS","fontSize":18,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"01 · 1.1 — THE FULL stop_reason SET & LOOP GUARDRAILS","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":18,"originalText":"01 · 1.1 — THE FULL stop_reason SET & LOOP GUARDRAILS","height":24,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":126,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":16,"angle":0,"y":630,"width":1118},{"seed":128,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-127","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"WHAT: Part A taught tool_use vs end_turn. The exam can test ALL the stop_reason values. Branching on stop_reason is the ONLY correct way to decide what the\nloop does next.\n\n• end_turn — natural finish. Exit the loop and return the text.\n• tool_use — Claude wants tools run. Append the assistant turn verbatim, run each tool, append ONE user turn made only of tool_result blocks (each with its\ntool_use_id), call again.\n• max_tokens — hit the output cap. It may have been cut off in the MIDDLE of a tool_use block. If the last block is tool_use, retry with a HIGHER max_tokens\n(retrying with the same limit fails again). Otherwise ask to continue or warn about truncation.\n• stop_sequence — matched one of your custom stop strings. Usually a successful stop.\n• pause_turn — server-side tools (web search, code execution) hit their iteration cap. Append the assistant response UNCHANGED and call again. Do NOT invent\na tool_result.\n• refusal — Claude declined for safety. Do not keep looping: surface it, rephrase, route elsewhere, or escalate.\n• model_context_window_exceeded — the whole context window filled (not max_tokens). Treat like max_tokens: continue, summarize or compact.\n\nGUARDRAILS (not the main stop): max_turns / maxTurns and max_budget_usd cap runaway loops. When tripped, the Agent SDK returns a result with subtype\nerror_max_turns or error_max_budget_usd. They protect you; they are NOT how a healthy loop ends.\n\nAGENT SDK VIEW: query() runs the loop for you and streams messages. 'assistant' messages carry each turn; a final 'result' message ends it (subtype\n'success' or an error subtype). The loop ends when an assistant message has no tool_use blocks.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'response cut off mid tool call' → max_tokens, raise the limit. 'long server-side search stopped' → pause_turn, re-send. 'model declined' →\nrefusal, don't loop.","fontSize":13,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"WHAT: Part A taught tool_use vs end_turn. The exam can test ALL the stop_reason values. Branching on stop_reason is the ONLY correct way to decide what the\nloop does next.\n\n• end_turn — natural finish. Exit the loop and return the text.\n• tool_use — Claude wants tools run. Append the assistant turn verbatim, run each tool, append ONE user turn made only of tool_result blocks (each with its\ntool_use_id), call again.\n• max_tokens — hit the output cap. It may have been cut off in the MIDDLE of a tool_use block. If the last block is tool_use, retry with a HIGHER max_tokens\n(retrying with the same limit fails again). Otherwise ask to continue or warn about truncation.\n• stop_sequence — matched one of your custom stop strings. Usually a successful stop.\n• pause_turn — server-side tools (web search, code execution) hit their iteration cap. Append the assistant response UNCHANGED and call again. Do NOT invent\na tool_result.\n• refusal — Claude declined for safety. Do not keep looping: surface it, rephrase, route elsewhere, or escalate.\n• model_context_window_exceeded — the whole context window filled (not max_tokens). Treat like max_tokens: continue, summarize or compact.\n\nGUARDRAILS (not the main stop): max_turns / maxTurns and max_budget_usd cap runaway loops. When tripped, the Agent SDK returns a result with subtype\nerror_max_turns or error_max_budget_usd. They protect you; they are NOT how a healthy loop ends.\n\nAGENT SDK VIEW: query() runs the loop for you and streams messages. 'assistant' messages carry each turn; a final 'result' message ends it (subtype\n'success' or an error subtype). The loop ends when an assistant message has no tool_use blocks.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'response cut off mid tool call' → max_tokens, raise the limit. 'long server-side search stopped' → pause_turn, re-send. 'model declined' →\nrefusal, don't loop.","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":13,"originalText":"WHAT: Part A taught tool_use vs end_turn. The exam can test ALL the stop_reason values. Branching on stop_reason is the ONLY correct way to decide what the\nloop does next.\n\n• end_turn — natural finish. Exit the loop and return the text.\n• tool_use — Claude wants tools run. Append the assistant turn verbatim, run each tool, append ONE user turn made only of tool_result blocks (each with its\ntool_use_id), call again.\n• max_tokens — hit the output cap. It may have been cut off in the MIDDLE of a tool_use block. If the last block is tool_use, retry with a HIGHER max_tokens\n(retrying with the same limit fails again). Otherwise ask to continue or warn about truncation.\n• stop_sequence — matched one of your custom stop strings. Usually a successful stop.\n• pause_turn — server-side tools (web search, code execution) hit their iteration cap. Append the assistant response UNCHANGED and call again. Do NOT invent\na tool_result.\n• refusal — Claude declined for safety. Do not keep looping: surface it, rephrase, route elsewhere, or escalate.\n• model_context_window_exceeded — the whole context window filled (not max_tokens). Treat like max_tokens: continue, summarize or compact.\n\nGUARDRAILS (not the main stop): max_turns / maxTurns and max_budget_usd cap runaway loops. When tripped, the Agent SDK returns a result with subtype\nerror_max_turns or error_max_budget_usd. They protect you; they are NOT how a healthy loop ends.\n\nAGENT SDK VIEW: query() runs the loop for you and streams messages. 'assistant' messages carry each turn; a final 'result' message ends it (subtype\n'success' or an error subtype). The loop ends when an assistant message has no tool_use blocks.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'response cut off mid tool call' → max_tokens, raise the limit. 'long server-side search stopped' → pause_turn, re-send. 'model declined' →\nrefusal, don't loop.","height":377.52000000000004,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":129,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":16,"angle":0,"y":662,"width":1118},{"isDeleted":false,"locked":false,"strokeColor":"#2457c5","link":null,"roughness":1,"groupIds":[],"backgroundColor":"#f1f3f9","angle":0,"fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"version":1,"roundness":{"type":3},"type":"rectangle","updated":1791909281572,"id":"r-130","x":1190,"seed":131,"boundElements":null,"y":618,"opacity":100,"height":372.88,"width":1150,"frameId":null,"strokeStyle":"solid","versionNonce":132},{"seed":134,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-133","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"02 · 1.1 — AGENT vs WORKFLOW, AND LOOP ANTI-PATTERNS","fontSize":18,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"02 · 1.1 — AGENT vs WORKFLOW, AND LOOP ANTI-PATTERNS","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":18,"originalText":"02 · 1.1 — AGENT vs WORKFLOW, AND LOOP ANTI-PATTERNS","height":24,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":135,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":1206,"angle":0,"y":630,"width":1118},{"seed":137,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-136","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"AGENT = the model decides the next tool from the running context (model-driven). WORKFLOW = YOUR code fixes the sequence (a pre-configured decision tree).\nPick the simpler workflow when the path is known in advance; pick an agent when the path is open-ended.\n\nANTI-PATTERNS the exam loves (each looks reasonable but is wrong):\n• Parsing the text for 'DONE' or 'Final answer' to stop — brittle; the model may still want another tool.\n• Using an iteration cap (for _ in range(10)) as the PRIMARY stop — cuts off hard tasks, wastes tokens on easy ones. Caps are guardrails only.\n• Deciding 'done' because text is present — a turn can hold text AND a tool_use block. Check stop_reason and loop over blocks by type.\n• Not appending the assistant turn before sending tool_results — invalid messages array, API error.\n• Adding extra text blocks after the tool_results in the user turn — can teach Claude to expect user text and cause empty end_turn replies. That user turn\nshould contain only tool_result blocks.\n• Treating pause_turn like end_turn — truncates server-tool work.\n• Hardcoding the tool sequence and expecting agent-like recovery — that's a workflow, not an agent.\n\nEXAMPLE: A loop prints 'TASK COMPLETE' in the text and the code stops there, but the same response also contained a tool_use block for a refund. The refund\nnever runs. Fix: stop only when stop_reason == end_turn (no tool_use blocks).\n\nEXAM TELLS: they show code or a description and ask which line is the bug — look for text-parsing, a cap used as the stop, a missing assistant turn, or\nignored pause_turn.","fontSize":13,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"AGENT = the model decides the next tool from the running context (model-driven). WORKFLOW = YOUR code fixes the sequence (a pre-configured decision tree).\nPick the simpler workflow when the path is known in advance; pick an agent when the path is open-ended.\n\nANTI-PATTERNS the exam loves (each looks reasonable but is wrong):\n• Parsing the text for 'DONE' or 'Final answer' to stop — brittle; the model may still want another tool.\n• Using an iteration cap (for _ in range(10)) as the PRIMARY stop — cuts off hard tasks, wastes tokens on easy ones. Caps are guardrails only.\n• Deciding 'done' because text is present — a turn can hold text AND a tool_use block. Check stop_reason and loop over blocks by type.\n• Not appending the assistant turn before sending tool_results — invalid messages array, API error.\n• Adding extra text blocks after the tool_results in the user turn — can teach Claude to expect user text and cause empty end_turn replies. That user turn\nshould contain only tool_result blocks.\n• Treating pause_turn like end_turn — truncates server-tool work.\n• Hardcoding the tool sequence and expecting agent-like recovery — that's a workflow, not an agent.\n\nEXAMPLE: A loop prints 'TASK COMPLETE' in the text and the code stops there, but the same response also contained a tool_use block for a refund. The refund\nnever runs. Fix: stop only when stop_reason == end_turn (no tool_use blocks).\n\nEXAM TELLS: they show code or a description and ask which line is the bug — look for text-parsing, a cap used as the stop, a missing assistant turn, or\nignored pause_turn.","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":13,"originalText":"AGENT = the model decides the next tool from the running context (model-driven). WORKFLOW = YOUR code fixes the sequence (a pre-configured decision tree).\nPick the simpler workflow when the path is known in advance; pick an agent when the path is open-ended.\n\nANTI-PATTERNS the exam loves (each looks reasonable but is wrong):\n• Parsing the text for 'DONE' or 'Final answer' to stop — brittle; the model may still want another tool.\n• Using an iteration cap (for _ in range(10)) as the PRIMARY stop — cuts off hard tasks, wastes tokens on easy ones. Caps are guardrails only.\n• Deciding 'done' because text is present — a turn can hold text AND a tool_use block. Check stop_reason and loop over blocks by type.\n• Not appending the assistant turn before sending tool_results — invalid messages array, API error.\n• Adding extra text blocks after the tool_results in the user turn — can teach Claude to expect user text and cause empty end_turn replies. That user turn\nshould contain only tool_result blocks.\n• Treating pause_turn like end_turn — truncates server-tool work.\n• Hardcoding the tool sequence and expecting agent-like recovery — that's a workflow, not an agent.\n\nEXAMPLE: A loop prints 'TASK COMPLETE' in the text and the code stops there, but the same response also contained a tool_use block for a refund. The refund\nnever runs. Fix: stop only when stop_reason == end_turn (no tool_use blocks).\n\nEXAM TELLS: they show code or a description and ask which line is the bug — look for text-parsing, a cap used as the stop, a missing assistant turn, or\nignored pause_turn.","height":308.88,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":138,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":1206,"angle":0,"y":662,"width":1118},{"isDeleted":false,"locked":false,"strokeColor":"#2457c5","link":null,"roughness":1,"groupIds":[],"backgroundColor":"#f1f3f9","angle":0,"fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"version":1,"roundness":{"type":3},"type":"rectangle","updated":1791909281572,"id":"r-139","x":0,"seed":140,"boundElements":null,"y":1087.52,"opacity":100,"height":407.2,"width":1150,"frameId":null,"strokeStyle":"solid","versionNonce":141},{"seed":143,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-142","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"03 · 1.2 — HUB-AND-SPOKE, DECOMPOSITION COVERAGE & ITERATIVE REFINEMENT","fontSize":18,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"03 · 1.2 — HUB-AND-SPOKE, DECOMPOSITION COVERAGE & ITERATIVE REFINEMENT","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":18,"originalText":"03 · 1.2 — HUB-AND-SPOKE, DECOMPOSITION COVERAGE & ITERATIVE REFINEMENT","height":24,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":144,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":16,"angle":0,"y":1099.52,"width":1118},{"seed":146,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-145","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"TOPOLOGY: coordinator/subagent systems are HUB-AND-SPOKE. One coordinator handles ALL communication, error handling and routing. Subagents never talk to\neach other directly.\n\nWHY IT'S GOOD: (1) OBSERVABILITY — every action passes through one node, so coordinator logs show the full cause-and-effect trail. (2) BOUNDED BLAST RADIUS\n— a faulty subagent only corrupts its own context; the hub sees just its final message and can reject or re-delegate.\n\nCOORDINATOR DUTIES: decompose the task, delegate, aggregate results, and CHOOSE subagents dynamically based on query complexity (don't push every query\nthrough the full pipeline).\n\n★ THE CLASSIC TRAP — OVERLY NARROW DECOMPOSITION: A coordinator splits 'creative industries' into digital art, graphic design and photography. Every\nsubagent succeeds, yet music, writing and film are missing. The ROOT CAUSE is the coordinator's decomposition — not the subagents. Fix: check domain breadth\nBEFORE delegating, audit for gaps AFTER synthesis, and re-delegate for the gaps (iterative refinement).\n\nOTHER SKILLS TESTED: partition scope so subagents don't duplicate work; route all calls through the coordinator; re-delegate when synthesis reveals a gap.\n\nCOST FACT: multi-agent systems use roughly 3–15× the tokens of a single agent. Worth it only for broad, parallelizable, high-value work (ties to Part A:\nstart single-agent).\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'all subagents completed successfully but the report is missing whole topics' → coordinator decomposition (not subagent quality, not a bigger\nmodel).","fontSize":13,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"TOPOLOGY: coordinator/subagent systems are HUB-AND-SPOKE. One coordinator handles ALL communication, error handling and routing. Subagents never talk to\neach other directly.\n\nWHY IT'S GOOD: (1) OBSERVABILITY — every action passes through one node, so coordinator logs show the full cause-and-effect trail. (2) BOUNDED BLAST RADIUS\n— a faulty subagent only corrupts its own context; the hub sees just its final message and can reject or re-delegate.\n\nCOORDINATOR DUTIES: decompose the task, delegate, aggregate results, and CHOOSE subagents dynamically based on query complexity (don't push every query\nthrough the full pipeline).\n\n★ THE CLASSIC TRAP — OVERLY NARROW DECOMPOSITION: A coordinator splits 'creative industries' into digital art, graphic design and photography. Every\nsubagent succeeds, yet music, writing and film are missing. The ROOT CAUSE is the coordinator's decomposition — not the subagents. Fix: check domain breadth\nBEFORE delegating, audit for gaps AFTER synthesis, and re-delegate for the gaps (iterative refinement).\n\nOTHER SKILLS TESTED: partition scope so subagents don't duplicate work; route all calls through the coordinator; re-delegate when synthesis reveals a gap.\n\nCOST FACT: multi-agent systems use roughly 3–15× the tokens of a single agent. Worth it only for broad, parallelizable, high-value work (ties to Part A:\nstart single-agent).\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'all subagents completed successfully but the report is missing whole topics' → coordinator decomposition (not subagent quality, not a bigger\nmodel).","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":13,"originalText":"TOPOLOGY: coordinator/subagent systems are HUB-AND-SPOKE. One coordinator handles ALL communication, error handling and routing. Subagents never talk to\neach other directly.\n\nWHY IT'S GOOD: (1) OBSERVABILITY — every action passes through one node, so coordinator logs show the full cause-and-effect trail. (2) BOUNDED BLAST RADIUS\n— a faulty subagent only corrupts its own context; the hub sees just its final message and can reject or re-delegate.\n\nCOORDINATOR DUTIES: decompose the task, delegate, aggregate results, and CHOOSE subagents dynamically based on query complexity (don't push every query\nthrough the full pipeline).\n\n★ THE CLASSIC TRAP — OVERLY NARROW DECOMPOSITION: A coordinator splits 'creative industries' into digital art, graphic design and photography. Every\nsubagent succeeds, yet music, writing and film are missing. The ROOT CAUSE is the coordinator's decomposition — not the subagents. Fix: check domain breadth\nBEFORE delegating, audit for gaps AFTER synthesis, and re-delegate for the gaps (iterative refinement).\n\nOTHER SKILLS TESTED: partition scope so subagents don't duplicate work; route all calls through the coordinator; re-delegate when synthesis reveals a gap.\n\nCOST FACT: multi-agent systems use roughly 3–15× the tokens of a single agent. Worth it only for broad, parallelizable, high-value work (ties to Part A:\nstart single-agent).\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'all subagents completed successfully but the report is missing whole topics' → coordinator decomposition (not subagent quality, not a bigger\nmodel).","height":343.2,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":147,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":16,"angle":0,"y":1131.52,"width":1118},{"isDeleted":false,"locked":false,"strokeColor":"#2457c5","link":null,"roughness":1,"groupIds":[],"backgroundColor":"#f1f3f9","angle":0,"fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"version":1,"roundness":{"type":3},"type":"rectangle","updated":1791909281572,"id":"r-148","x":1190,"seed":149,"boundElements":null,"y":1087.52,"opacity":100,"height":475.84000000000003,"width":1150,"frameId":null,"strokeStyle":"solid","versionNonce":150},{"seed":152,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-151","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"04 · 1.3 — INVOKING SUBAGENTS: THE Task (AGENT) TOOL & AgentDefinition","fontSize":18,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"04 · 1.3 — INVOKING SUBAGENTS: THE Task (AGENT) TOOL & AgentDefinition","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":18,"originalText":"04 · 1.3 — INVOKING SUBAGENTS: THE Task (AGENT) TOOL & AgentDefinition","height":24,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":153,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":1206,"angle":0,"y":1099.52,"width":1118},{"seed":155,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-154","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"HOW A SUBAGENT STARTS: the coordinator calls the Task tool (renamed 'Agent' in newer Claude Code versions; treat Task as the exam name, and know production\ncode may see either).\n\nRULES THE EXAM TESTS:\n• Task (Agent) must be in the COORDINATOR'S allowedTools — otherwise nothing can be spawned.\n• NEVER put Task/Agent in a subagent's tools — that would allow subagents to spawn subagents (uncontrolled recursion).\n\nAgentDefinition — how you describe each subagent:\n• description — WHEN the coordinator should invoke it (this is what the coordinator reads to choose).\n• prompt — the subagent's own system prompt.\n• tools / disallowedTools — restrict what it can do (least privilege).\n• optional: model (e.g., a cheaper model for simple work), skills, mcpServers, permissionMode.\n\nWHAT A SUBAGENT RECEIVES: its AgentDefinition prompt + the string in the Task call + its allowed tool definitions (+ project CLAUDE.md if enabled). WHAT IT\nDOES NOT RECEIVE: the parent conversation, the parent's tool results, the parent system prompt, or the parent's memory.\n\nCONSEQUENCES: (1) only the subagent's FINAL message returns, so a subagent can read dozens of files and hand back a short summary (compression). (2) The\ncoordinator MUST forward anything needed — file paths, earlier findings, user constraints. (3) No back-channel between subagents; shared state goes to a\nfile/external store and references are passed back.\n\nEXAMPLE: web-researcher (WebSearch, WebFetch, Read) and doc-analyst (Read, Grep, Glob) are defined with narrow tools; the coordinator's allowedTools\nincludes Task.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'subagent invents URLs / repeats searches' → it was starved of context. 'nothing spawns' → Task missing from allowedTools.","fontSize":13,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"HOW A SUBAGENT STARTS: the coordinator calls the Task tool (renamed 'Agent' in newer Claude Code versions; treat Task as the exam name, and know production\ncode may see either).\n\nRULES THE EXAM TESTS:\n• Task (Agent) must be in the COORDINATOR'S allowedTools — otherwise nothing can be spawned.\n• NEVER put Task/Agent in a subagent's tools — that would allow subagents to spawn subagents (uncontrolled recursion).\n\nAgentDefinition — how you describe each subagent:\n• description — WHEN the coordinator should invoke it (this is what the coordinator reads to choose).\n• prompt — the subagent's own system prompt.\n• tools / disallowedTools — restrict what it can do (least privilege).\n• optional: model (e.g., a cheaper model for simple work), skills, mcpServers, permissionMode.\n\nWHAT A SUBAGENT RECEIVES: its AgentDefinition prompt + the string in the Task call + its allowed tool definitions (+ project CLAUDE.md if enabled). WHAT IT\nDOES NOT RECEIVE: the parent conversation, the parent's tool results, the parent system prompt, or the parent's memory.\n\nCONSEQUENCES: (1) only the subagent's FINAL message returns, so a subagent can read dozens of files and hand back a short summary (compression). (2) The\ncoordinator MUST forward anything needed — file paths, earlier findings, user constraints. (3) No back-channel between subagents; shared state goes to a\nfile/external store and references are passed back.\n\nEXAMPLE: web-researcher (WebSearch, WebFetch, Read) and doc-analyst (Read, Grep, Glob) are defined with narrow tools; the coordinator's allowedTools\nincludes Task.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'subagent invents URLs / repeats searches' → it was starved of context. 'nothing spawns' → Task missing from allowedTools.","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":13,"originalText":"HOW A SUBAGENT STARTS: the coordinator calls the Task tool (renamed 'Agent' in newer Claude Code versions; treat Task as the exam name, and know production\ncode may see either).\n\nRULES THE EXAM TESTS:\n• Task (Agent) must be in the COORDINATOR'S allowedTools — otherwise nothing can be spawned.\n• NEVER put Task/Agent in a subagent's tools — that would allow subagents to spawn subagents (uncontrolled recursion).\n\nAgentDefinition — how you describe each subagent:\n• description — WHEN the coordinator should invoke it (this is what the coordinator reads to choose).\n• prompt — the subagent's own system prompt.\n• tools / disallowedTools — restrict what it can do (least privilege).\n• optional: model (e.g., a cheaper model for simple work), skills, mcpServers, permissionMode.\n\nWHAT A SUBAGENT RECEIVES: its AgentDefinition prompt + the string in the Task call + its allowed tool definitions (+ project CLAUDE.md if enabled). WHAT IT\nDOES NOT RECEIVE: the parent conversation, the parent's tool results, the parent system prompt, or the parent's memory.\n\nCONSEQUENCES: (1) only the subagent's FINAL message returns, so a subagent can read dozens of files and hand back a short summary (compression). (2) The\ncoordinator MUST forward anything needed — file paths, earlier findings, user constraints. (3) No back-channel between subagents; shared state goes to a\nfile/external store and references are passed back.\n\nEXAMPLE: web-researcher (WebSearch, WebFetch, Read) and doc-analyst (Read, Grep, Glob) are defined with narrow tools; the coordinator's allowedTools\nincludes Task.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'subagent invents URLs / repeats searches' → it was starved of context. 'nothing spawns' → Task missing from allowedTools.","height":411.84000000000003,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":156,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":1206,"angle":0,"y":1131.52,"width":1118},{"isDeleted":false,"locked":false,"strokeColor":"#2457c5","link":null,"roughness":1,"groupIds":[],"backgroundColor":"#f1f3f9","angle":0,"fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"version":1,"roundness":{"type":3},"type":"rectangle","updated":1791909281572,"id":"r-157","x":0,"seed":158,"boundElements":null,"y":1591.3600000000001,"opacity":100,"height":407.2,"width":1150,"frameId":null,"strokeStyle":"solid","versionNonce":159},{"seed":161,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-160","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"05 · 1.3 — CONTEXT PASSING, PARALLEL SPAWNING & HOW TO PROMPT SUBAGENTS","fontSize":18,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"05 · 1.3 — CONTEXT PASSING, PARALLEL SPAWNING & HOW TO PROMPT SUBAGENTS","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":18,"originalText":"05 · 1.3 — CONTEXT PASSING, PARALLEL SPAWNING & HOW TO PROMPT SUBAGENTS","height":24,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":162,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":16,"angle":0,"y":1603.3600000000001,"width":1118},{"seed":164,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-163","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"PASS CONTEXT EXPLICITLY AND IN STRUCTURE. Separate CONTENT from METADATA so attribution survives each handoff. A good briefing contains: the task + goal\n(with a quality bar), prior_findings (claim, source_url, source_title, page, retrieved_at, confidence), open_questions (so the subagent doesn't redo work),\nan output_format schema the coordinator can check, and explicit 'do not' lines.\n\nWHY STRUCTURED: URLs, document names and page numbers survive, so the final synthesis can cite precisely instead of paraphrasing. A schema lets the\ncoordinator machine-check the result before accepting it.\n\nPARALLEL SPAWNING: parallelism means MULTIPLE Task calls in ONE coordinator response (one assistant turn). Calling them one per turn is sequential and is\nthe failure mode. Reported effect: 3–5 parallel subagents, each making several parallel tool calls, can cut research time dramatically.\n\nHOW TO PROMPT SUBAGENTS: give GOALS and QUALITY CRITERIA, not step-by-step procedures. Vague instructions cause duplication and gaps. The biggest quality\ngains came from stating objective, output format, tool guidance and task boundaries.\n\nfork_session (preview of 1.7): used when subagents/branches should start from a shared baseline and try different approaches — it is NOT the way to do\nparallel decomposition of different subtasks.\n\nLONG RUNS: a lead coordinator can lose its plan after very long runs. Save the plan to memory/file early; on context pressure, start a fresh coordinator\nwith the plan and a summary.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'research time too slow' → emit all Task calls in one turn. 'citations lost' → structured context with metadata.","fontSize":13,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"PASS CONTEXT EXPLICITLY AND IN STRUCTURE. Separate CONTENT from METADATA so attribution survives each handoff. A good briefing contains: the task + goal\n(with a quality bar), prior_findings (claim, source_url, source_title, page, retrieved_at, confidence), open_questions (so the subagent doesn't redo work),\nan output_format schema the coordinator can check, and explicit 'do not' lines.\n\nWHY STRUCTURED: URLs, document names and page numbers survive, so the final synthesis can cite precisely instead of paraphrasing. A schema lets the\ncoordinator machine-check the result before accepting it.\n\nPARALLEL SPAWNING: parallelism means MULTIPLE Task calls in ONE coordinator response (one assistant turn). Calling them one per turn is sequential and is\nthe failure mode. Reported effect: 3–5 parallel subagents, each making several parallel tool calls, can cut research time dramatically.\n\nHOW TO PROMPT SUBAGENTS: give GOALS and QUALITY CRITERIA, not step-by-step procedures. Vague instructions cause duplication and gaps. The biggest quality\ngains came from stating objective, output format, tool guidance and task boundaries.\n\nfork_session (preview of 1.7): used when subagents/branches should start from a shared baseline and try different approaches — it is NOT the way to do\nparallel decomposition of different subtasks.\n\nLONG RUNS: a lead coordinator can lose its plan after very long runs. Save the plan to memory/file early; on context pressure, start a fresh coordinator\nwith the plan and a summary.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'research time too slow' → emit all Task calls in one turn. 'citations lost' → structured context with metadata.","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":13,"originalText":"PASS CONTEXT EXPLICITLY AND IN STRUCTURE. Separate CONTENT from METADATA so attribution survives each handoff. A good briefing contains: the task + goal\n(with a quality bar), prior_findings (claim, source_url, source_title, page, retrieved_at, confidence), open_questions (so the subagent doesn't redo work),\nan output_format schema the coordinator can check, and explicit 'do not' lines.\n\nWHY STRUCTURED: URLs, document names and page numbers survive, so the final synthesis can cite precisely instead of paraphrasing. A schema lets the\ncoordinator machine-check the result before accepting it.\n\nPARALLEL SPAWNING: parallelism means MULTIPLE Task calls in ONE coordinator response (one assistant turn). Calling them one per turn is sequential and is\nthe failure mode. Reported effect: 3–5 parallel subagents, each making several parallel tool calls, can cut research time dramatically.\n\nHOW TO PROMPT SUBAGENTS: give GOALS and QUALITY CRITERIA, not step-by-step procedures. Vague instructions cause duplication and gaps. The biggest quality\ngains came from stating objective, output format, tool guidance and task boundaries.\n\nfork_session (preview of 1.7): used when subagents/branches should start from a shared baseline and try different approaches — it is NOT the way to do\nparallel decomposition of different subtasks.\n\nLONG RUNS: a lead coordinator can lose its plan after very long runs. Save the plan to memory/file early; on context pressure, start a fresh coordinator\nwith the plan and a summary.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'research time too slow' → emit all Task calls in one turn. 'citations lost' → structured context with metadata.","height":343.2,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":165,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":16,"angle":0,"y":1635.3600000000001,"width":1118},{"isDeleted":false,"locked":false,"strokeColor":"#2457c5","link":null,"roughness":1,"groupIds":[],"backgroundColor":"#f1f3f9","angle":0,"fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"version":1,"roundness":{"type":3},"type":"rectangle","updated":1791909281572,"id":"r-166","x":1190,"seed":167,"boundElements":null,"y":1591.3600000000001,"opacity":100,"height":441.52000000000004,"width":1150,"frameId":null,"strokeStyle":"solid","versionNonce":168},{"seed":170,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-169","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"06 · 1.4 — PROGRAMMATIC ENFORCEMENT vs PROMPT GUIDANCE","fontSize":18,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"06 · 1.4 — PROGRAMMATIC ENFORCEMENT vs PROMPT GUIDANCE","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":18,"originalText":"06 · 1.4 — PROGRAMMATIC ENFORCEMENT vs PROMPT GUIDANCE","height":24,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":171,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":1206,"angle":0,"y":1603.3600000000001,"width":1118},{"seed":173,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-172","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"THE IDEA: a prompt is advice with a non-zero failure rate. Where a mistake is costly, irreversible or regulated, put the rule in CODE that can block the\naction.\n\nCANONICAL EXAMPLE: never run process_refund until get_customer has returned a VERIFIED customer ID. A prompt line 'verify the customer first' can be skipped\nafter prompt drift or a model upgrade. A PreToolUse hook (or an equivalent gate in your orchestration code) records verified IDs and DENIES process_refund\nfor anyone not yet verified, returning a reason that tells the model to verify first.\n\nDECISION TABLE:\n• Style, tone, formatting → prompt (a gate is overkill).\n• Preferred tool order, not compliance-bound → prompt is fine.\n• Identity check before a financial action → programmatic gate.\n• Writes to protected paths → programmatic gate.\n• Audit logging → PostToolUse hook.\n• Prerequisites across subagents → code/hook state shared across subagent start/stop.\n• Approval routing to a human → permission/approval mechanism, not a polite instruction.\n\nRULE OF THUMB: if breaking the rule would be a serious incident, enforce it in code.\n\nALSO TESTED: split a multi-concern request (e.g., a refund AND an address change) into parallel investigations that share context, then merge.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'must ALWAYS / must NEVER / compliance / financial' → programmatic enforcement. 'add stronger wording', 'add CAPS' → wrong (still\nprobabilistic).","fontSize":13,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"THE IDEA: a prompt is advice with a non-zero failure rate. Where a mistake is costly, irreversible or regulated, put the rule in CODE that can block the\naction.\n\nCANONICAL EXAMPLE: never run process_refund until get_customer has returned a VERIFIED customer ID. A prompt line 'verify the customer first' can be skipped\nafter prompt drift or a model upgrade. A PreToolUse hook (or an equivalent gate in your orchestration code) records verified IDs and DENIES process_refund\nfor anyone not yet verified, returning a reason that tells the model to verify first.\n\nDECISION TABLE:\n• Style, tone, formatting → prompt (a gate is overkill).\n• Preferred tool order, not compliance-bound → prompt is fine.\n• Identity check before a financial action → programmatic gate.\n• Writes to protected paths → programmatic gate.\n• Audit logging → PostToolUse hook.\n• Prerequisites across subagents → code/hook state shared across subagent start/stop.\n• Approval routing to a human → permission/approval mechanism, not a polite instruction.\n\nRULE OF THUMB: if breaking the rule would be a serious incident, enforce it in code.\n\nALSO TESTED: split a multi-concern request (e.g., a refund AND an address change) into parallel investigations that share context, then merge.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'must ALWAYS / must NEVER / compliance / financial' → programmatic enforcement. 'add stronger wording', 'add CAPS' → wrong (still\nprobabilistic).","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":13,"originalText":"THE IDEA: a prompt is advice with a non-zero failure rate. Where a mistake is costly, irreversible or regulated, put the rule in CODE that can block the\naction.\n\nCANONICAL EXAMPLE: never run process_refund until get_customer has returned a VERIFIED customer ID. A prompt line 'verify the customer first' can be skipped\nafter prompt drift or a model upgrade. A PreToolUse hook (or an equivalent gate in your orchestration code) records verified IDs and DENIES process_refund\nfor anyone not yet verified, returning a reason that tells the model to verify first.\n\nDECISION TABLE:\n• Style, tone, formatting → prompt (a gate is overkill).\n• Preferred tool order, not compliance-bound → prompt is fine.\n• Identity check before a financial action → programmatic gate.\n• Writes to protected paths → programmatic gate.\n• Audit logging → PostToolUse hook.\n• Prerequisites across subagents → code/hook state shared across subagent start/stop.\n• Approval routing to a human → permission/approval mechanism, not a polite instruction.\n\nRULE OF THUMB: if breaking the rule would be a serious incident, enforce it in code.\n\nALSO TESTED: split a multi-concern request (e.g., a refund AND an address change) into parallel investigations that share context, then merge.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'must ALWAYS / must NEVER / compliance / financial' → programmatic enforcement. 'add stronger wording', 'add CAPS' → wrong (still\nprobabilistic).","height":377.52000000000004,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":174,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":1206,"angle":0,"y":1635.3600000000001,"width":1118},{"isDeleted":false,"locked":false,"strokeColor":"#2457c5","link":null,"roughness":1,"groupIds":[],"backgroundColor":"#f1f3f9","angle":0,"fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"version":1,"roundness":{"type":3},"type":"rectangle","updated":1791909281572,"id":"r-175","x":0,"seed":176,"boundElements":null,"y":2060.88,"opacity":100,"height":407.2,"width":1150,"frameId":null,"strokeStyle":"solid","versionNonce":177},{"seed":179,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-178","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"07 · 1.4 — STRUCTURED HANDOFF TO A HUMAN","fontSize":18,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"07 · 1.4 — STRUCTURED HANDOFF TO A HUMAN","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":18,"originalText":"07 · 1.4 — STRUCTURED HANDOFF TO A HUMAN","height":24,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":180,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":16,"angle":0,"y":2072.88,"width":1118},{"seed":182,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-181","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"THE PROBLEM: the human agent who receives an escalation does NOT have the transcript. If the handoff is a vague 'customer is upset', the human must re-\nverify, re-investigate and may contradict what was already done.\n\nA GOOD HANDOFF CONTAINS:\n• type, reason, urgency (e.g., human_escalation / policy_exception_required / medium).\n• customer: ID, verified status, how and when verified (so no re-verification).\n• case: ticket ID and each concern with its status (so no redoing lookups).\n• root_cause: summary + evidence references like log/event IDs (so no re-investigating).\n• attempted_actions: every tool call with its result and any gate that blocked it (so nobody repeats or undoes).\n• recommended_action: concrete next steps.\n• policy_refs: which policy/playbook applies (so decisions are consistent).\n\nVALIDATE THE SCHEMA at the escalation boundary so an incomplete handoff can't leave the system.\n\nThe same structure works for subagent → coordinator escalation: return partial results plus what was attempted, not just 'failed'.\n\nEXAMPLE: refund over the limit. Handoff says: customer C-881 verified by order+email at 10:02; refund $740 of order O-5521; root cause: item arrived damaged\n(event E-33); gate blocked refund > $500; recommend manager approval per policy REF-12.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'human agents keep re-asking the customer' / 'escalations lack context' → structured handoff summary, not 'forward the whole transcript'.","fontSize":13,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"THE PROBLEM: the human agent who receives an escalation does NOT have the transcript. If the handoff is a vague 'customer is upset', the human must re-\nverify, re-investigate and may contradict what was already done.\n\nA GOOD HANDOFF CONTAINS:\n• type, reason, urgency (e.g., human_escalation / policy_exception_required / medium).\n• customer: ID, verified status, how and when verified (so no re-verification).\n• case: ticket ID and each concern with its status (so no redoing lookups).\n• root_cause: summary + evidence references like log/event IDs (so no re-investigating).\n• attempted_actions: every tool call with its result and any gate that blocked it (so nobody repeats or undoes).\n• recommended_action: concrete next steps.\n• policy_refs: which policy/playbook applies (so decisions are consistent).\n\nVALIDATE THE SCHEMA at the escalation boundary so an incomplete handoff can't leave the system.\n\nThe same structure works for subagent → coordinator escalation: return partial results plus what was attempted, not just 'failed'.\n\nEXAMPLE: refund over the limit. Handoff says: customer C-881 verified by order+email at 10:02; refund $740 of order O-5521; root cause: item arrived damaged\n(event E-33); gate blocked refund > $500; recommend manager approval per policy REF-12.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'human agents keep re-asking the customer' / 'escalations lack context' → structured handoff summary, not 'forward the whole transcript'.","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":13,"originalText":"THE PROBLEM: the human agent who receives an escalation does NOT have the transcript. If the handoff is a vague 'customer is upset', the human must re-\nverify, re-investigate and may contradict what was already done.\n\nA GOOD HANDOFF CONTAINS:\n• type, reason, urgency (e.g., human_escalation / policy_exception_required / medium).\n• customer: ID, verified status, how and when verified (so no re-verification).\n• case: ticket ID and each concern with its status (so no redoing lookups).\n• root_cause: summary + evidence references like log/event IDs (so no re-investigating).\n• attempted_actions: every tool call with its result and any gate that blocked it (so nobody repeats or undoes).\n• recommended_action: concrete next steps.\n• policy_refs: which policy/playbook applies (so decisions are consistent).\n\nVALIDATE THE SCHEMA at the escalation boundary so an incomplete handoff can't leave the system.\n\nThe same structure works for subagent → coordinator escalation: return partial results plus what was attempted, not just 'failed'.\n\nEXAMPLE: refund over the limit. Handoff says: customer C-881 verified by order+email at 10:02; refund $740 of order O-5521; root cause: item arrived damaged\n(event E-33); gate blocked refund > $500; recommend manager approval per policy REF-12.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'human agents keep re-asking the customer' / 'escalations lack context' → structured handoff summary, not 'forward the whole transcript'.","height":343.2,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":183,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":16,"angle":0,"y":2104.88,"width":1118},{"isDeleted":false,"locked":false,"strokeColor":"#2457c5","link":null,"roughness":1,"groupIds":[],"backgroundColor":"#f1f3f9","angle":0,"fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"version":1,"roundness":{"type":3},"type":"rectangle","updated":1791909281572,"id":"r-184","x":1190,"seed":185,"boundElements":null,"y":2060.88,"opacity":100,"height":441.52000000000004,"width":1150,"frameId":null,"strokeStyle":"solid","versionNonce":186},{"seed":188,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-187","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"08 · 1.5 — SDK HOOKS: WHAT THEY ARE & THE KEY EVENTS","fontSize":18,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"08 · 1.5 — SDK HOOKS: WHAT THEY ARE & THE KEY EVENTS","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":18,"originalText":"08 · 1.5 — SDK HOOKS: WHAT THEY ARE & THE KEY EVENTS","height":24,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":189,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":1206,"angle":0,"y":2072.88,"width":1118},{"seed":191,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-190","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"WHAT: a hook is YOUR code that runs automatically at a defined point in the agent's lifecycle. Hooks give DETERMINISTIC guarantees; prompts give only\nprobabilistic compliance. If it must always happen, use a hook.\n\nTHE TWO YOU MUST KNOW COLD:\n• PreToolUse — fires BEFORE a tool call runs. Can ALLOW, DENY, ASK or DEFER the call, rewrite the input, or redirect to an alternative. Think: policy\nenforcement, blocking, sandboxing.\n• PostToolUse — fires AFTER a tool call succeeds, BEFORE the model sees the result. Can transform/normalize the result or append context. Think: data\nnormalization, audit logging, format conversion.\n\nOTHER EVENTS (know they exist and roughly why):\n• UserPromptSubmit — before the model sees a user prompt (inject context, scrub PII, block off-topic).\n• PostToolUseFailure — after a tool fails (custom error handling).\n• SubagentStart / SubagentStop — a subagent spawns/finishes (track parallel work, aggregate results).\n• SessionStart / SessionEnd — session lifecycle (init logging, cleanup). In the SDK, TypeScript supports these as callbacks; in Python they are shell hooks\nin settings.\n• PreCompact / PostCompact — around context compaction (archive before the lossy summary).\n• Stop — the turn ends (save state, alerts). PermissionRequest — approval flow. Notification — forward alerts.\n\nTWO WAYS TO REGISTER: (1) SDK callbacks in your options (ClaudeAgentOptions.hooks in Python, options.hooks in TypeScript); (2) shell-command hooks in\n.claude/settings.json — run as child processes and receive event JSON on stdin.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'transform results from several servers before the model sees them' → PostToolUse. 'block an action by policy' → PreToolUse.","fontSize":13,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"WHAT: a hook is YOUR code that runs automatically at a defined point in the agent's lifecycle. Hooks give DETERMINISTIC guarantees; prompts give only\nprobabilistic compliance. If it must always happen, use a hook.\n\nTHE TWO YOU MUST KNOW COLD:\n• PreToolUse — fires BEFORE a tool call runs. Can ALLOW, DENY, ASK or DEFER the call, rewrite the input, or redirect to an alternative. Think: policy\nenforcement, blocking, sandboxing.\n• PostToolUse — fires AFTER a tool call succeeds, BEFORE the model sees the result. Can transform/normalize the result or append context. Think: data\nnormalization, audit logging, format conversion.\n\nOTHER EVENTS (know they exist and roughly why):\n• UserPromptSubmit — before the model sees a user prompt (inject context, scrub PII, block off-topic).\n• PostToolUseFailure — after a tool fails (custom error handling).\n• SubagentStart / SubagentStop — a subagent spawns/finishes (track parallel work, aggregate results).\n• SessionStart / SessionEnd — session lifecycle (init logging, cleanup). In the SDK, TypeScript supports these as callbacks; in Python they are shell hooks\nin settings.\n• PreCompact / PostCompact — around context compaction (archive before the lossy summary).\n• Stop — the turn ends (save state, alerts). PermissionRequest — approval flow. Notification — forward alerts.\n\nTWO WAYS TO REGISTER: (1) SDK callbacks in your options (ClaudeAgentOptions.hooks in Python, options.hooks in TypeScript); (2) shell-command hooks in\n.claude/settings.json — run as child processes and receive event JSON on stdin.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'transform results from several servers before the model sees them' → PostToolUse. 'block an action by policy' → PreToolUse.","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":13,"originalText":"WHAT: a hook is YOUR code that runs automatically at a defined point in the agent's lifecycle. Hooks give DETERMINISTIC guarantees; prompts give only\nprobabilistic compliance. If it must always happen, use a hook.\n\nTHE TWO YOU MUST KNOW COLD:\n• PreToolUse — fires BEFORE a tool call runs. Can ALLOW, DENY, ASK or DEFER the call, rewrite the input, or redirect to an alternative. Think: policy\nenforcement, blocking, sandboxing.\n• PostToolUse — fires AFTER a tool call succeeds, BEFORE the model sees the result. Can transform/normalize the result or append context. Think: data\nnormalization, audit logging, format conversion.\n\nOTHER EVENTS (know they exist and roughly why):\n• UserPromptSubmit — before the model sees a user prompt (inject context, scrub PII, block off-topic).\n• PostToolUseFailure — after a tool fails (custom error handling).\n• SubagentStart / SubagentStop — a subagent spawns/finishes (track parallel work, aggregate results).\n• SessionStart / SessionEnd — session lifecycle (init logging, cleanup). In the SDK, TypeScript supports these as callbacks; in Python they are shell hooks\nin settings.\n• PreCompact / PostCompact — around context compaction (archive before the lossy summary).\n• Stop — the turn ends (save state, alerts). PermissionRequest — approval flow. Notification — forward alerts.\n\nTWO WAYS TO REGISTER: (1) SDK callbacks in your options (ClaudeAgentOptions.hooks in Python, options.hooks in TypeScript); (2) shell-command hooks in\n.claude/settings.json — run as child processes and receive event JSON on stdin.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'transform results from several servers before the model sees them' → PostToolUse. 'block an action by policy' → PreToolUse.","height":377.52000000000004,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":192,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":1206,"angle":0,"y":2104.88,"width":1118},{"isDeleted":false,"locked":false,"strokeColor":"#2457c5","link":null,"roughness":1,"groupIds":[],"backgroundColor":"#f1f3f9","angle":0,"fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"version":1,"roundness":{"type":3},"type":"rectangle","updated":1791909281572,"id":"r-193","x":0,"seed":194,"boundElements":null,"y":2530.4,"opacity":100,"height":475.84000000000003,"width":1150,"frameId":null,"strokeStyle":"solid","versionNonce":195},{"seed":197,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-196","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"09 · 1.5 — HOOK MECHANICS: MATCHERS, OUTPUTS, EXIT CODES, PRIORITY","fontSize":18,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"09 · 1.5 — HOOK MECHANICS: MATCHERS, OUTPUTS, EXIT CODES, PRIORITY","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":18,"originalText":"09 · 1.5 — HOOK MECHANICS: MATCHERS, OUTPUTS, EXIT CODES, PRIORITY","height":24,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":198,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":16,"angle":0,"y":2542.4,"width":1118},{"seed":200,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-199","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"MATCHERS (which tool a hook applies to):\n• '*', empty or omitted → all tools.\n• Only letters, digits and | → exact name or a pipe list, e.g. Edit|Write.\n• Anything else → treated as a regular expression, e.g. ^mcp__memory__ for all tools of one MCP server.\n• Tool hooks match on the TOOL NAME only. To filter on a file path or argument, check it inside the handler.\n\nWHAT A HOOK RECEIVES: session_id, cwd, hook_event_name, plus event data such as tool_name, tool_input and tool_response (agent_id/agent_type inside a\nsubagent).\n\nWHAT A HOOK CAN RETURN:\n• PreToolUse: permissionDecision = allow | deny | ask | defer, a permissionDecisionReason, and optionally updatedInput (rewrites the tool's input — only\ntakes effect when the decision is allow).\n• PostToolUse: updatedToolOutput REPLACES what the model sees; additionalContext APPENDS to it.\n• General: systemMessage, continue (stop or not), additionalContext.\n\nSHELL HOOK EXIT CODES: 0 = success (stdout parsed as JSON); 2 = BLOCKING error (stderr goes to the model; for PreToolUse the call is blocked); any other\nnon-zero = non-blocking error.\n\nPRIORITY WHEN SEVERAL HOOKS DISAGREE: deny > defer > ask > allow. A single deny blocks the call.\n\nWHY THE REASON TEXT MATTERS: a good permissionDecisionReason names the allowed alternative ('use create_escalation instead'), so the agent self-corrects\ninstead of retrying the denied call.\n\nEXAM TELLS: replace vs append → updatedToolOutput vs additionalContext. Exit code 2 = block. Deny always wins.","fontSize":13,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"MATCHERS (which tool a hook applies to):\n• '*', empty or omitted → all tools.\n• Only letters, digits and | → exact name or a pipe list, e.g. Edit|Write.\n• Anything else → treated as a regular expression, e.g. ^mcp__memory__ for all tools of one MCP server.\n• Tool hooks match on the TOOL NAME only. To filter on a file path or argument, check it inside the handler.\n\nWHAT A HOOK RECEIVES: session_id, cwd, hook_event_name, plus event data such as tool_name, tool_input and tool_response (agent_id/agent_type inside a\nsubagent).\n\nWHAT A HOOK CAN RETURN:\n• PreToolUse: permissionDecision = allow | deny | ask | defer, a permissionDecisionReason, and optionally updatedInput (rewrites the tool's input — only\ntakes effect when the decision is allow).\n• PostToolUse: updatedToolOutput REPLACES what the model sees; additionalContext APPENDS to it.\n• General: systemMessage, continue (stop or not), additionalContext.\n\nSHELL HOOK EXIT CODES: 0 = success (stdout parsed as JSON); 2 = BLOCKING error (stderr goes to the model; for PreToolUse the call is blocked); any other\nnon-zero = non-blocking error.\n\nPRIORITY WHEN SEVERAL HOOKS DISAGREE: deny > defer > ask > allow. A single deny blocks the call.\n\nWHY THE REASON TEXT MATTERS: a good permissionDecisionReason names the allowed alternative ('use create_escalation instead'), so the agent self-corrects\ninstead of retrying the denied call.\n\nEXAM TELLS: replace vs append → updatedToolOutput vs additionalContext. Exit code 2 = block. Deny always wins.","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":13,"originalText":"MATCHERS (which tool a hook applies to):\n• '*', empty or omitted → all tools.\n• Only letters, digits and | → exact name or a pipe list, e.g. Edit|Write.\n• Anything else → treated as a regular expression, e.g. ^mcp__memory__ for all tools of one MCP server.\n• Tool hooks match on the TOOL NAME only. To filter on a file path or argument, check it inside the handler.\n\nWHAT A HOOK RECEIVES: session_id, cwd, hook_event_name, plus event data such as tool_name, tool_input and tool_response (agent_id/agent_type inside a\nsubagent).\n\nWHAT A HOOK CAN RETURN:\n• PreToolUse: permissionDecision = allow | deny | ask | defer, a permissionDecisionReason, and optionally updatedInput (rewrites the tool's input — only\ntakes effect when the decision is allow).\n• PostToolUse: updatedToolOutput REPLACES what the model sees; additionalContext APPENDS to it.\n• General: systemMessage, continue (stop or not), additionalContext.\n\nSHELL HOOK EXIT CODES: 0 = success (stdout parsed as JSON); 2 = BLOCKING error (stderr goes to the model; for PreToolUse the call is blocked); any other\nnon-zero = non-blocking error.\n\nPRIORITY WHEN SEVERAL HOOKS DISAGREE: deny > defer > ask > allow. A single deny blocks the call.\n\nWHY THE REASON TEXT MATTERS: a good permissionDecisionReason names the allowed alternative ('use create_escalation instead'), so the agent self-corrects\ninstead of retrying the denied call.\n\nEXAM TELLS: replace vs append → updatedToolOutput vs additionalContext. Exit code 2 = block. Deny always wins.","height":411.84000000000003,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":201,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":16,"angle":0,"y":2574.4,"width":1118},{"isDeleted":false,"locked":false,"strokeColor":"#2457c5","link":null,"roughness":1,"groupIds":[],"backgroundColor":"#f1f3f9","angle":0,"fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"version":1,"roundness":{"type":3},"type":"rectangle","updated":1791909281572,"id":"r-202","x":1190,"seed":203,"boundElements":null,"y":2530.4,"opacity":100,"height":407.2,"width":1150,"frameId":null,"strokeStyle":"solid","versionNonce":204},{"seed":206,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-205","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"10 · 1.5 — HOOK EXAMPLES & WHEN NOT TO USE A HOOK","fontSize":18,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"10 · 1.5 — HOOK EXAMPLES & WHEN NOT TO USE A HOOK","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":18,"originalText":"10 · 1.5 — HOOK EXAMPLES & WHEN NOT TO USE A HOOK","height":24,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":207,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":1206,"angle":0,"y":2542.4,"width":1118},{"seed":209,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-208","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"EXAMPLE 1 — PostToolUse NORMALIZATION: three MCP servers return dates as Unix seconds, ISO 8601 strings and millisecond integers. A PostToolUse hook\n(matcher ^mcp__) converts all of them to ISO 8601 and returns updatedToolOutput, so the model always sees one consistent format. (Domain 2 link: a hook, not\na prompt, fixes heterogeneous results.)\n\nEXAMPLE 2 — PreToolUse POLICY GUARD: refunds above $500 must not go through process_refund. A PreToolUse hook on that tool denies when amount > 500, with a\nreason pointing to create_escalation. The model receives the reason and escalates instead of retrying.\n\nEXAMPLE 3 — shell hook: matcher 'Edit|Write' on PostToolUse runs a formatter (like Prettier) on the changed file; a PreToolUse hook on Bash blocks rm -rf\npatterns.\n\nUSE A HOOK FOR: hard regulatory rules, data-shape contracts, audit logs, per-call quota/cost caps, anything where a miss is a P0 incident.\nUSE A PROMPT FOR: soft style, tone, persona, preferences.\n\nA HOOK IS NOT: a way to make Claude 'smarter'. It cannot fix a missing field the tool never returned (that's a tool-design gap, Part A). It guarantees\nbehavior at defined points.\n\nCOMMON CONFUSION: PostToolUse normalizes what comes INTO the model (tool output). PreToolUse controls what goes OUT to tools. Mixing them up is the classic\nwrong answer.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'must be guaranteed', 'compliance', 'across multiple servers with different formats' → hook.","fontSize":13,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"EXAMPLE 1 — PostToolUse NORMALIZATION: three MCP servers return dates as Unix seconds, ISO 8601 strings and millisecond integers. A PostToolUse hook\n(matcher ^mcp__) converts all of them to ISO 8601 and returns updatedToolOutput, so the model always sees one consistent format. (Domain 2 link: a hook, not\na prompt, fixes heterogeneous results.)\n\nEXAMPLE 2 — PreToolUse POLICY GUARD: refunds above $500 must not go through process_refund. A PreToolUse hook on that tool denies when amount > 500, with a\nreason pointing to create_escalation. The model receives the reason and escalates instead of retrying.\n\nEXAMPLE 3 — shell hook: matcher 'Edit|Write' on PostToolUse runs a formatter (like Prettier) on the changed file; a PreToolUse hook on Bash blocks rm -rf\npatterns.\n\nUSE A HOOK FOR: hard regulatory rules, data-shape contracts, audit logs, per-call quota/cost caps, anything where a miss is a P0 incident.\nUSE A PROMPT FOR: soft style, tone, persona, preferences.\n\nA HOOK IS NOT: a way to make Claude 'smarter'. It cannot fix a missing field the tool never returned (that's a tool-design gap, Part A). It guarantees\nbehavior at defined points.\n\nCOMMON CONFUSION: PostToolUse normalizes what comes INTO the model (tool output). PreToolUse controls what goes OUT to tools. Mixing them up is the classic\nwrong answer.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'must be guaranteed', 'compliance', 'across multiple servers with different formats' → hook.","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":13,"originalText":"EXAMPLE 1 — PostToolUse NORMALIZATION: three MCP servers return dates as Unix seconds, ISO 8601 strings and millisecond integers. A PostToolUse hook\n(matcher ^mcp__) converts all of them to ISO 8601 and returns updatedToolOutput, so the model always sees one consistent format. (Domain 2 link: a hook, not\na prompt, fixes heterogeneous results.)\n\nEXAMPLE 2 — PreToolUse POLICY GUARD: refunds above $500 must not go through process_refund. A PreToolUse hook on that tool denies when amount > 500, with a\nreason pointing to create_escalation. The model receives the reason and escalates instead of retrying.\n\nEXAMPLE 3 — shell hook: matcher 'Edit|Write' on PostToolUse runs a formatter (like Prettier) on the changed file; a PreToolUse hook on Bash blocks rm -rf\npatterns.\n\nUSE A HOOK FOR: hard regulatory rules, data-shape contracts, audit logs, per-call quota/cost caps, anything where a miss is a P0 incident.\nUSE A PROMPT FOR: soft style, tone, persona, preferences.\n\nA HOOK IS NOT: a way to make Claude 'smarter'. It cannot fix a missing field the tool never returned (that's a tool-design gap, Part A). It guarantees\nbehavior at defined points.\n\nCOMMON CONFUSION: PostToolUse normalizes what comes INTO the model (tool output). PreToolUse controls what goes OUT to tools. Mixing them up is the classic\nwrong answer.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'must be guaranteed', 'compliance', 'across multiple servers with different formats' → hook.","height":343.2,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":210,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":1206,"angle":0,"y":2574.4,"width":1118},{"isDeleted":false,"locked":false,"strokeColor":"#2457c5","link":null,"roughness":1,"groupIds":[],"backgroundColor":"#f1f3f9","angle":0,"fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"version":1,"roundness":{"type":3},"type":"rectangle","updated":1791909281572,"id":"r-211","x":0,"seed":212,"boundElements":null,"y":3034.2400000000002,"opacity":100,"height":407.2,"width":1150,"frameId":null,"strokeStyle":"solid","versionNonce":213},{"seed":215,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-214","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"11 · 1.6 — PROMPT CHAINING vs DYNAMIC ADAPTIVE DECOMPOSITION","fontSize":18,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"11 · 1.6 — PROMPT CHAINING vs DYNAMIC ADAPTIVE DECOMPOSITION","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":18,"originalText":"11 · 1.6 — PROMPT CHAINING vs DYNAMIC ADAPTIVE DECOMPOSITION","height":24,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":216,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":16,"angle":0,"y":3046.2400000000002,"width":1118},{"seed":218,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-217","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"PROMPT CHAINING: a FIXED sequence of N steps where each call feeds the next. Use it for predictable workflows whose steps are known up front. It trades a\nbit of latency for accuracy because each call is simpler. Add programmatic GATES between steps to fail fast (e.g., outline → check against a rubric →\nwrite).\n\nDYNAMIC ADAPTIVE DECOMPOSITION (orchestrator-workers): the orchestrator decides the subtasks AT RUNTIME, spawns workers, and synthesizes. Use it when the\nnumber or shape of subtasks depends on the input and is only discovered while working. In the Agent SDK this is the Task/subagent pattern.\n\nWORKFLOW-SHAPE CHEAT SHEET:\n• Steps known in advance, same each time → prompt chaining.\n• Independent subtasks of known shape → parallelization (sectioning).\n• Same task run N times to vote for confidence → parallelization (voting).\n• Distinct categories, each with a specialist → routing.\n• Subtask count/shape depends on the input → orchestrator-workers (adaptive).\n• Output improves with critique → evaluator-optimizer loop.\n• Open-ended, multi-turn, unknown length → full agent loop.\n\nHOW THIS RELATES TO PART A: chain = ordering, coordinator = splitting (adaptive), peer-to-peer = checking. Part B adds the vocabulary the exam uses for the\nsame ideas.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'steps are the same for every document' → chaining. 'we don't know how many pieces until we explore' → adaptive/orchestrator-workers.","fontSize":13,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"PROMPT CHAINING: a FIXED sequence of N steps where each call feeds the next. Use it for predictable workflows whose steps are known up front. It trades a\nbit of latency for accuracy because each call is simpler. Add programmatic GATES between steps to fail fast (e.g., outline → check against a rubric →\nwrite).\n\nDYNAMIC ADAPTIVE DECOMPOSITION (orchestrator-workers): the orchestrator decides the subtasks AT RUNTIME, spawns workers, and synthesizes. Use it when the\nnumber or shape of subtasks depends on the input and is only discovered while working. In the Agent SDK this is the Task/subagent pattern.\n\nWORKFLOW-SHAPE CHEAT SHEET:\n• Steps known in advance, same each time → prompt chaining.\n• Independent subtasks of known shape → parallelization (sectioning).\n• Same task run N times to vote for confidence → parallelization (voting).\n• Distinct categories, each with a specialist → routing.\n• Subtask count/shape depends on the input → orchestrator-workers (adaptive).\n• Output improves with critique → evaluator-optimizer loop.\n• Open-ended, multi-turn, unknown length → full agent loop.\n\nHOW THIS RELATES TO PART A: chain = ordering, coordinator = splitting (adaptive), peer-to-peer = checking. Part B adds the vocabulary the exam uses for the\nsame ideas.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'steps are the same for every document' → chaining. 'we don't know how many pieces until we explore' → adaptive/orchestrator-workers.","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":13,"originalText":"PROMPT CHAINING: a FIXED sequence of N steps where each call feeds the next. Use it for predictable workflows whose steps are known up front. It trades a\nbit of latency for accuracy because each call is simpler. Add programmatic GATES between steps to fail fast (e.g., outline → check against a rubric →\nwrite).\n\nDYNAMIC ADAPTIVE DECOMPOSITION (orchestrator-workers): the orchestrator decides the subtasks AT RUNTIME, spawns workers, and synthesizes. Use it when the\nnumber or shape of subtasks depends on the input and is only discovered while working. In the Agent SDK this is the Task/subagent pattern.\n\nWORKFLOW-SHAPE CHEAT SHEET:\n• Steps known in advance, same each time → prompt chaining.\n• Independent subtasks of known shape → parallelization (sectioning).\n• Same task run N times to vote for confidence → parallelization (voting).\n• Distinct categories, each with a specialist → routing.\n• Subtask count/shape depends on the input → orchestrator-workers (adaptive).\n• Output improves with critique → evaluator-optimizer loop.\n• Open-ended, multi-turn, unknown length → full agent loop.\n\nHOW THIS RELATES TO PART A: chain = ordering, coordinator = splitting (adaptive), peer-to-peer = checking. Part B adds the vocabulary the exam uses for the\nsame ideas.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'steps are the same for every document' → chaining. 'we don't know how many pieces until we explore' → adaptive/orchestrator-workers.","height":343.2,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":219,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":16,"angle":0,"y":3078.2400000000002,"width":1118},{"isDeleted":false,"locked":false,"strokeColor":"#2457c5","link":null,"roughness":1,"groupIds":[],"backgroundColor":"#f1f3f9","angle":0,"fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"version":1,"roundness":{"type":3},"type":"rectangle","updated":1791909281572,"id":"r-220","x":1190,"seed":221,"boundElements":null,"y":3034.2400000000002,"opacity":100,"height":372.88,"width":1150,"frameId":null,"strokeStyle":"solid","versionNonce":222},{"seed":224,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-223","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"12 · 1.6 — WORKED EXAMPLES: LARGE CODE REVIEW & LEGACY TESTS","fontSize":18,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"12 · 1.6 — WORKED EXAMPLES: LARGE CODE REVIEW & LEGACY TESTS","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":18,"originalText":"12 · 1.6 — WORKED EXAMPLES: LARGE CODE REVIEW & LEGACY TESTS","height":24,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":225,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":1206,"angle":0,"y":3046.2400000000002,"width":1118},{"seed":227,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-226","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"★ LARGE PR (say 40 files) — TWO-PASS DESIGN to avoid attention dilution (one giant pass spreads Claude's attention too thin and misses issues):\nPASS 1 — PER-FILE (parallelizable): review each file on its own with a focused prompt and limited tools (e.g., Read, Grep), returning structured findings\n(file + issues). Each per-file review can run as a fork of a shared baseline session that already loaded CLAUDE.md, the PR description and the diff summary,\nwith a turn cap (max_turns).\nPASS 2 — ONE CROSS-FILE CALL: collect all per-file findings and make a single integration pass for cross-cutting problems: API contract drift, type\nmismatches, missing call-site updates, multi-file security holes.\nPATTERN: chaining (pass 1 → pass 2) with parallelization inside pass 1. Per-file passes catch local issues; the cross-file pass catches interactions.\n\nWHY NOT ONE BIG PASS: attention dilution — too much at once, shallow analysis, inconsistent depth. WHY NOT ONLY PER-FILE: it cannot see issues that span\nfiles.\n\nOPEN-ENDED EXAMPLE — 'add tests to a legacy codebase': this is ADAPTIVE, not chained. The agent maps the structure, finds high-impact untested modules,\nbuilds a prioritized backlog, writes tests for the top item, and pushes newly discovered dependencies back onto the backlog. You can't script the steps in\nadvance.\n\nDECISION: known fixed steps → chain. Subtasks discovered while working → adaptive. Too much for one pass → split per-item then synthesize across items.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'review misses bugs on a very large change' → per-file passes + cross-file pass. 'upgrade to a bigger context window' → distractor.","fontSize":13,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"★ LARGE PR (say 40 files) — TWO-PASS DESIGN to avoid attention dilution (one giant pass spreads Claude's attention too thin and misses issues):\nPASS 1 — PER-FILE (parallelizable): review each file on its own with a focused prompt and limited tools (e.g., Read, Grep), returning structured findings\n(file + issues). Each per-file review can run as a fork of a shared baseline session that already loaded CLAUDE.md, the PR description and the diff summary,\nwith a turn cap (max_turns).\nPASS 2 — ONE CROSS-FILE CALL: collect all per-file findings and make a single integration pass for cross-cutting problems: API contract drift, type\nmismatches, missing call-site updates, multi-file security holes.\nPATTERN: chaining (pass 1 → pass 2) with parallelization inside pass 1. Per-file passes catch local issues; the cross-file pass catches interactions.\n\nWHY NOT ONE BIG PASS: attention dilution — too much at once, shallow analysis, inconsistent depth. WHY NOT ONLY PER-FILE: it cannot see issues that span\nfiles.\n\nOPEN-ENDED EXAMPLE — 'add tests to a legacy codebase': this is ADAPTIVE, not chained. The agent maps the structure, finds high-impact untested modules,\nbuilds a prioritized backlog, writes tests for the top item, and pushes newly discovered dependencies back onto the backlog. You can't script the steps in\nadvance.\n\nDECISION: known fixed steps → chain. Subtasks discovered while working → adaptive. Too much for one pass → split per-item then synthesize across items.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'review misses bugs on a very large change' → per-file passes + cross-file pass. 'upgrade to a bigger context window' → distractor.","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":13,"originalText":"★ LARGE PR (say 40 files) — TWO-PASS DESIGN to avoid attention dilution (one giant pass spreads Claude's attention too thin and misses issues):\nPASS 1 — PER-FILE (parallelizable): review each file on its own with a focused prompt and limited tools (e.g., Read, Grep), returning structured findings\n(file + issues). Each per-file review can run as a fork of a shared baseline session that already loaded CLAUDE.md, the PR description and the diff summary,\nwith a turn cap (max_turns).\nPASS 2 — ONE CROSS-FILE CALL: collect all per-file findings and make a single integration pass for cross-cutting problems: API contract drift, type\nmismatches, missing call-site updates, multi-file security holes.\nPATTERN: chaining (pass 1 → pass 2) with parallelization inside pass 1. Per-file passes catch local issues; the cross-file pass catches interactions.\n\nWHY NOT ONE BIG PASS: attention dilution — too much at once, shallow analysis, inconsistent depth. WHY NOT ONLY PER-FILE: it cannot see issues that span\nfiles.\n\nOPEN-ENDED EXAMPLE — 'add tests to a legacy codebase': this is ADAPTIVE, not chained. The agent maps the structure, finds high-impact untested modules,\nbuilds a prioritized backlog, writes tests for the top item, and pushes newly discovered dependencies back onto the backlog. You can't script the steps in\nadvance.\n\nDECISION: known fixed steps → chain. Subtasks discovered while working → adaptive. Too much for one pass → split per-item then synthesize across items.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'review misses bugs on a very large change' → per-file passes + cross-file pass. 'upgrade to a bigger context window' → distractor.","height":308.88,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":228,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":1206,"angle":0,"y":3078.2400000000002,"width":1118},{"isDeleted":false,"locked":false,"strokeColor":"#2457c5","link":null,"roughness":1,"groupIds":[],"backgroundColor":"#f1f3f9","angle":0,"fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"version":1,"roundness":{"type":3},"type":"rectangle","updated":1791909281572,"id":"r-229","x":0,"seed":230,"boundElements":null,"y":3469.44,"opacity":100,"height":390.04,"width":1150,"frameId":null,"strokeStyle":"solid","versionNonce":231},{"seed":233,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-232","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"13 · 1.7 — SESSIONS: RESUME, CONTINUE, NAMES, STORAGE","fontSize":18,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"13 · 1.7 — SESSIONS: RESUME, CONTINUE, NAMES, STORAGE","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":18,"originalText":"13 · 1.7 — SESSIONS: RESUME, CONTINUE, NAMES, STORAGE","height":24,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":234,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":16,"angle":0,"y":3481.44,"width":1118},{"seed":236,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-235","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"A SESSION is a saved conversation. Claude Code stores each at ~/.claude/projects/<slugified-cwd>/<session-id>.jsonl (the slug is the working directory path\nwith non-alphanumeric characters turned into '-').\n\nCHOOSING A MODE:\n• claude -c / --continue (SDK: continue: true) — the MOST RECENT session in the CURRENT directory. Takes no ID.\n• claude -r / --resume <id-or-name> (SDK: resume: '<id>') — a SPECIFIC session by ID or by the name you gave it.\n• claude (plain) — a brand-new session with a new ID.\n• --name / -n gives a session a name you can resume later. --session-id sets an explicit ID.\n• TypeScript option persistSession: false keeps a session in memory only (no disk file).\n\nCAPTURE THE SESSION ID: if you plan to resume from code, save the ID every run — in Python from the result message's session_id; in TypeScript from the\nresult message or the init system message.\n\n★ #1 CAUSE OF 'RESUME STARTED A FRESH SESSION': you are in a DIFFERENT working directory (cwd). The session file lives under the slug of the original cwd,\nso it isn't found and a fresh session begins silently.\n\nPATTERN: keep a long-lived named session for stable context (for example a design-review session) and fork it for each investigation.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'resume didn't restore the conversation' → check the cwd. '--continue with an ID' → wrong; continue takes no ID.","fontSize":13,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"A SESSION is a saved conversation. Claude Code stores each at ~/.claude/projects/<slugified-cwd>/<session-id>.jsonl (the slug is the working directory path\nwith non-alphanumeric characters turned into '-').\n\nCHOOSING A MODE:\n• claude -c / --continue (SDK: continue: true) — the MOST RECENT session in the CURRENT directory. Takes no ID.\n• claude -r / --resume <id-or-name> (SDK: resume: '<id>') — a SPECIFIC session by ID or by the name you gave it.\n• claude (plain) — a brand-new session with a new ID.\n• --name / -n gives a session a name you can resume later. --session-id sets an explicit ID.\n• TypeScript option persistSession: false keeps a session in memory only (no disk file).\n\nCAPTURE THE SESSION ID: if you plan to resume from code, save the ID every run — in Python from the result message's session_id; in TypeScript from the\nresult message or the init system message.\n\n★ #1 CAUSE OF 'RESUME STARTED A FRESH SESSION': you are in a DIFFERENT working directory (cwd). The session file lives under the slug of the original cwd,\nso it isn't found and a fresh session begins silently.\n\nPATTERN: keep a long-lived named session for stable context (for example a design-review session) and fork it for each investigation.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'resume didn't restore the conversation' → check the cwd. '--continue with an ID' → wrong; continue takes no ID.","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":13,"originalText":"A SESSION is a saved conversation. Claude Code stores each at ~/.claude/projects/<slugified-cwd>/<session-id>.jsonl (the slug is the working directory path\nwith non-alphanumeric characters turned into '-').\n\nCHOOSING A MODE:\n• claude -c / --continue (SDK: continue: true) — the MOST RECENT session in the CURRENT directory. Takes no ID.\n• claude -r / --resume <id-or-name> (SDK: resume: '<id>') — a SPECIFIC session by ID or by the name you gave it.\n• claude (plain) — a brand-new session with a new ID.\n• --name / -n gives a session a name you can resume later. --session-id sets an explicit ID.\n• TypeScript option persistSession: false keeps a session in memory only (no disk file).\n\nCAPTURE THE SESSION ID: if you plan to resume from code, save the ID every run — in Python from the result message's session_id; in TypeScript from the\nresult message or the init system message.\n\n★ #1 CAUSE OF 'RESUME STARTED A FRESH SESSION': you are in a DIFFERENT working directory (cwd). The session file lives under the slug of the original cwd,\nso it isn't found and a fresh session begins silently.\n\nPATTERN: keep a long-lived named session for stable context (for example a design-review session) and fork it for each investigation.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'resume didn't restore the conversation' → check the cwd. '--continue with an ID' → wrong; continue takes no ID.","height":326.04,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":237,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":16,"angle":0,"y":3513.44,"width":1118},{"isDeleted":false,"locked":false,"strokeColor":"#2457c5","link":null,"roughness":1,"groupIds":[],"backgroundColor":"#f1f3f9","angle":0,"fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"version":1,"roundness":{"type":3},"type":"rectangle","updated":1791909281572,"id":"r-238","x":1190,"seed":239,"boundElements":null,"y":3469.44,"opacity":100,"height":475.84000000000003,"width":1150,"frameId":null,"strokeStyle":"solid","versionNonce":240},{"seed":242,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-241","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"14 · 1.7 — FORKING & STALE CONTEXT (WHEN NOT TO RESUME)","fontSize":18,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"14 · 1.7 — FORKING & STALE CONTEXT (WHEN NOT TO RESUME)","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":18,"originalText":"14 · 1.7 — FORKING & STALE CONTEXT (WHEN NOT TO RESUME)","height":24,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":243,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":1206,"angle":0,"y":3481.44,"width":1118},{"seed":245,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-244","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"FORKING (--fork-session; fork_session: true in Python, forkSession: true in TypeScript): copies the transcript so far into a NEW session ID. The original\nstays untouched. It requires resuming/continuing a session to fork from.\n\nWHEN TO FORK: compare two approaches from the same baseline (two refactors, two testing strategies), risky exploration with a safe parent to fall back on,\nper-item analysis that all start from the same loaded context.\n\n★ CAVEAT: a fork branches the CONVERSATION, not the FILESYSTEM. Two forks editing the same repo will collide. For isolated file changes use file\ncheckpointing or git worktrees (claude -w <name>).\n\nSTALE CONTEXT: a resumed session's earlier Read results are snapshots. If the files changed since, Claude may act on outdated content and even call\nfunctions that no longer exist.\n\nDECISION TREE:\n1) Did the earlier tool calls touch files that have since changed? NO → resume normally.\n2) YES, small surface, surgical edits → resume AND tell Claude exactly which files changed so it re-reads them.\n3) YES, large surface or broad changes → DISCARD the session and start FRESH with a written summary: (a) goal, (b) decisions made, (c) current code state,\n(d) open questions.\n\nSIMPLE RULE: fork to COMPARE, resume to CONTINUE, restart with a summary when context has gone STALE.\n\nDIFFERENT FROM: sub-agent parallel decomposition (different subtasks, Section 1.3/1.6). Fork = same starting point, different directions.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'files were refactored overnight, agent references old function' → stale context → restart with summary or resume + re-read. 'two forks\noverwrote each other' → forking doesn't isolate files; use worktrees.","fontSize":13,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"FORKING (--fork-session; fork_session: true in Python, forkSession: true in TypeScript): copies the transcript so far into a NEW session ID. The original\nstays untouched. It requires resuming/continuing a session to fork from.\n\nWHEN TO FORK: compare two approaches from the same baseline (two refactors, two testing strategies), risky exploration with a safe parent to fall back on,\nper-item analysis that all start from the same loaded context.\n\n★ CAVEAT: a fork branches the CONVERSATION, not the FILESYSTEM. Two forks editing the same repo will collide. For isolated file changes use file\ncheckpointing or git worktrees (claude -w <name>).\n\nSTALE CONTEXT: a resumed session's earlier Read results are snapshots. If the files changed since, Claude may act on outdated content and even call\nfunctions that no longer exist.\n\nDECISION TREE:\n1) Did the earlier tool calls touch files that have since changed? NO → resume normally.\n2) YES, small surface, surgical edits → resume AND tell Claude exactly which files changed so it re-reads them.\n3) YES, large surface or broad changes → DISCARD the session and start FRESH with a written summary: (a) goal, (b) decisions made, (c) current code state,\n(d) open questions.\n\nSIMPLE RULE: fork to COMPARE, resume to CONTINUE, restart with a summary when context has gone STALE.\n\nDIFFERENT FROM: sub-agent parallel decomposition (different subtasks, Section 1.3/1.6). Fork = same starting point, different directions.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'files were refactored overnight, agent references old function' → stale context → restart with summary or resume + re-read. 'two forks\noverwrote each other' → forking doesn't isolate files; use worktrees.","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":13,"originalText":"FORKING (--fork-session; fork_session: true in Python, forkSession: true in TypeScript): copies the transcript so far into a NEW session ID. The original\nstays untouched. It requires resuming/continuing a session to fork from.\n\nWHEN TO FORK: compare two approaches from the same baseline (two refactors, two testing strategies), risky exploration with a safe parent to fall back on,\nper-item analysis that all start from the same loaded context.\n\n★ CAVEAT: a fork branches the CONVERSATION, not the FILESYSTEM. Two forks editing the same repo will collide. For isolated file changes use file\ncheckpointing or git worktrees (claude -w <name>).\n\nSTALE CONTEXT: a resumed session's earlier Read results are snapshots. If the files changed since, Claude may act on outdated content and even call\nfunctions that no longer exist.\n\nDECISION TREE:\n1) Did the earlier tool calls touch files that have since changed? NO → resume normally.\n2) YES, small surface, surgical edits → resume AND tell Claude exactly which files changed so it re-reads them.\n3) YES, large surface or broad changes → DISCARD the session and start FRESH with a written summary: (a) goal, (b) decisions made, (c) current code state,\n(d) open questions.\n\nSIMPLE RULE: fork to COMPARE, resume to CONTINUE, restart with a summary when context has gone STALE.\n\nDIFFERENT FROM: sub-agent parallel decomposition (different subtasks, Section 1.3/1.6). Fork = same starting point, different directions.\n\nEXAM TELLS: 'files were refactored overnight, agent references old function' → stale context → restart with summary or resume + re-read. 'two forks\noverwrote each other' → forking doesn't isolate files; use worktrees.","height":411.84000000000003,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":246,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":1206,"angle":0,"y":3513.44,"width":1118},{"seed":248,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-247","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"HOOKS vs PROMPTS — THE THREE WAYS TO CONTROL BEHAVIOR","fontSize":26,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"HOOKS vs PROMPTS — THE THREE WAYS TO CONTROL BEHAVIOR","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":26,"originalText":"HOOKS vs PROMPTS — THE THREE WAYS TO CONTROL BEHAVIOR","height":36,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#0f3d8a","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":249,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":0,"angle":0,"y":3998.28,"width":2400},{"isDeleted":false,"locked":false,"strokeColor":"#2457c5","link":null,"roughness":1,"groupIds":[],"backgroundColor":"#a5d8ff","angle":0,"fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"version":1,"roundness":{"type":3},"type":"rectangle","updated":1791909281572,"id":"r-250","x":0,"seed":251,"boundElements":null,"y":4046.28,"opacity":100,"height":215.44,"width":745,"frameId":null,"strokeStyle":"solid","versionNonce":252},{"seed":254,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-253","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"PreToolUse — INTERCEPT BEFORE","fontSize":15,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"PreToolUse — INTERCEPT BEFORE","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":15,"originalText":"PreToolUse — INTERCEPT BEFORE","height":21,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":255,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":16,"angle":0,"y":4058.28,"width":713},{"seed":257,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-256","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"WHEN: before a tool call runs.\nCAN DO: allow / deny / ask / defer, rewrite input (updatedInput, needs allow), redirect.\nTYPICAL USE: policy, blocking, sandboxing, prerequisites.\nEXAMPLE: deny process_refund when amount > $500 or customer unverified.\nMATCH: tool name (regex or Edit|Write style).\nGUARANTEE? Yes — deterministic.\nDEFAULT WINNER: if hooks conflict, deny > defer > ask > allow.\nCOMMON MISTAKE: using it to clean tool OUTPUT (that's PostToolUse).\nONE WORD: GATE","fontSize":13,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"WHEN: before a tool call runs.\nCAN DO: allow / deny / ask / defer, rewrite input (updatedInput, needs allow), redirect.\nTYPICAL USE: policy, blocking, sandboxing, prerequisites.\nEXAMPLE: deny process_refund when amount > $500 or customer unverified.\nMATCH: tool name (regex or Edit|Write style).\nGUARANTEE? Yes — deterministic.\nDEFAULT WINNER: if hooks conflict, deny > defer > ask > allow.\nCOMMON MISTAKE: using it to clean tool OUTPUT (that's PostToolUse).\nONE WORD: GATE","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":13,"originalText":"WHEN: before a tool call runs.\nCAN DO: allow / deny / ask / defer, rewrite input (updatedInput, needs allow), redirect.\nTYPICAL USE: policy, blocking, sandboxing, prerequisites.\nEXAMPLE: deny process_refund when amount > $500 or customer unverified.\nMATCH: tool name (regex or Edit|Write style).\nGUARANTEE? Yes — deterministic.\nDEFAULT WINNER: if hooks conflict, deny > defer > ask > allow.\nCOMMON MISTAKE: using it to clean tool OUTPUT (that's PostToolUse).\nONE WORD: GATE","height":154.44,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":258,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":16,"angle":0,"y":4087.28,"width":713},{"isDeleted":false,"locked":false,"strokeColor":"#0f8a7c","link":null,"roughness":1,"groupIds":[],"backgroundColor":"#b2f2bb","angle":0,"fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"version":1,"roundness":{"type":3},"type":"rectangle","updated":1791909281572,"id":"r-259","x":775,"seed":260,"boundElements":null,"y":4046.28,"opacity":100,"height":215.44,"width":745,"frameId":null,"strokeStyle":"solid","versionNonce":261},{"seed":263,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-262","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"PostToolUse — TRANSFORM AFTER","fontSize":15,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"PostToolUse — TRANSFORM AFTER","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":15,"originalText":"PostToolUse — TRANSFORM AFTER","height":21,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":264,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":791,"angle":0,"y":4058.28,"width":713},{"seed":266,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-265","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"WHEN: after a tool succeeds, before the model sees the result.\nCAN DO: updatedToolOutput (replace) or additionalContext (append).\nTYPICAL USE: normalize formats, audit log, convert data.\nEXAMPLE: unify 3 timestamp formats from 3 MCP servers into ISO 8601.\nMATCH: tool name (e.g., ^mcp__).\nGUARANTEE? Yes — deterministic.\nNOTE: it cannot block the call (it already ran).\nCOMMON MISTAKE: expecting it to stop a bad call.\nONE WORD: CLEAN","fontSize":13,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"WHEN: after a tool succeeds, before the model sees the result.\nCAN DO: updatedToolOutput (replace) or additionalContext (append).\nTYPICAL USE: normalize formats, audit log, convert data.\nEXAMPLE: unify 3 timestamp formats from 3 MCP servers into ISO 8601.\nMATCH: tool name (e.g., ^mcp__).\nGUARANTEE? Yes — deterministic.\nNOTE: it cannot block the call (it already ran).\nCOMMON MISTAKE: expecting it to stop a bad call.\nONE WORD: CLEAN","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":13,"originalText":"WHEN: after a tool succeeds, before the model sees the result.\nCAN DO: updatedToolOutput (replace) or additionalContext (append).\nTYPICAL USE: normalize formats, audit log, convert data.\nEXAMPLE: unify 3 timestamp formats from 3 MCP servers into ISO 8601.\nMATCH: tool name (e.g., ^mcp__).\nGUARANTEE? Yes — deterministic.\nNOTE: it cannot block the call (it already ran).\nCOMMON MISTAKE: expecting it to stop a bad call.\nONE WORD: CLEAN","height":154.44,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":267,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":791,"angle":0,"y":4087.28,"width":713},{"isDeleted":false,"locked":false,"strokeColor":"#8a4fd1","link":null,"roughness":1,"groupIds":[],"backgroundColor":"#eebefa","angle":0,"fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"version":1,"roundness":{"type":3},"type":"rectangle","updated":1791909281572,"id":"r-268","x":1550,"seed":269,"boundElements":null,"y":4046.28,"opacity":100,"height":215.44,"width":745,"frameId":null,"strokeStyle":"solid","versionNonce":270},{"seed":272,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-271","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"PROMPT — ASK NICELY","fontSize":15,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"PROMPT — ASK NICELY","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":15,"originalText":"PROMPT — ASK NICELY","height":21,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":273,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":1566,"angle":0,"y":4058.28,"width":713},{"seed":275,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-274","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"WHEN: instructions in the system/user prompt.\nCAN DO: guide style, preferences, soft ordering.\nTYPICAL USE: tone, persona, formatting.\nEXAMPLE: 'Please verify the customer before refunding.'\nMATCH: n/a — the model decides whether to follow it.\nGUARANTEE? No — probabilistic.\nFAILURE: drifts after prompt edits or model upgrades.\nCOMMON MISTAKE: adding CAPS/'ALWAYS' instead of a gate.\nONE WORD: HOPE","fontSize":13,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"WHEN: instructions in the system/user prompt.\nCAN DO: guide style, preferences, soft ordering.\nTYPICAL USE: tone, persona, formatting.\nEXAMPLE: 'Please verify the customer before refunding.'\nMATCH: n/a — the model decides whether to follow it.\nGUARANTEE? No — probabilistic.\nFAILURE: drifts after prompt edits or model upgrades.\nCOMMON MISTAKE: adding CAPS/'ALWAYS' instead of a gate.\nONE WORD: HOPE","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":13,"originalText":"WHEN: instructions in the system/user prompt.\nCAN DO: guide style, preferences, soft ordering.\nTYPICAL USE: tone, persona, formatting.\nEXAMPLE: 'Please verify the customer before refunding.'\nMATCH: n/a — the model decides whether to follow it.\nGUARANTEE? No — probabilistic.\nFAILURE: drifts after prompt edits or model upgrades.\nCOMMON MISTAKE: adding CAPS/'ALWAYS' instead of a gate.\nONE WORD: HOPE","height":154.44,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":276,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":1566,"angle":0,"y":4087.28,"width":713},{"seed":278,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-277","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"★ DISCRIMINATORS — WHEN TWO ANSWERS LOOK RIGHT, THIS DECIDES IT","fontSize":26,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"★ DISCRIMINATORS — WHEN TWO ANSWERS LOOK RIGHT, THIS DECIDES IT","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":26,"originalText":"★ DISCRIMINATORS — WHEN TWO ANSWERS LOOK RIGHT, THIS DECIDES IT","height":36,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#0f3d8a","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":279,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":0,"angle":0,"y":4291.72,"width":2400},{"isDeleted":false,"locked":false,"strokeColor":"#b4303a","link":null,"roughness":1,"groupIds":[],"backgroundColor":"#fff0f0","angle":0,"fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"version":1,"roundness":{"type":3},"type":"rectangle","updated":1791909281572,"id":"r-280","x":0,"seed":281,"boundElements":null,"y":4339.72,"opacity":100,"height":251.76000000000002,"width":1150,"frameId":null,"strokeStyle":"solid","versionNonce":282},{"seed":284,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-283","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"1 · HOOK  vs  PROMPT","fontSize":17,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"1 · HOOK  vs  PROMPT","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":17,"originalText":"1 · HOOK  vs  PROMPT","height":23,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":285,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":16,"angle":0,"y":4351.72,"width":1118},{"seed":287,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-286","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"DECIDING QUESTION: Must this ALWAYS happen (or never), or is it a preference?\n\nLOOK FOR → HOOK: 'must always', 'compliance', 'regulated', 'financial', 'audit', 'cannot be skipped', 'after a model upgrade it stopped'. LOOK FOR → PROMPT:\n'tone', 'style', 'preferred', 'persona'.\nIf breaking the rule is a P0 incident, it goes in code.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Ask what happens if Claude ignores the rule once. Serious/irreversible → hook (or other programmatic gate). Cosmetic → prompt.\n\nEXAMPLE: Refunds require a verified customer. Put a PreToolUse gate on process_refund that denies until get_customer verified the ID.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Stronger wording ('ALWAYS', caps, repeated) is still probabilistic; it fails sometimes, and you can't prove it won't.","fontSize":13,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"DECIDING QUESTION: Must this ALWAYS happen (or never), or is it a preference?\n\nLOOK FOR → HOOK: 'must always', 'compliance', 'regulated', 'financial', 'audit', 'cannot be skipped', 'after a model upgrade it stopped'. LOOK FOR → PROMPT:\n'tone', 'style', 'preferred', 'persona'.\nIf breaking the rule is a P0 incident, it goes in code.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Ask what happens if Claude ignores the rule once. Serious/irreversible → hook (or other programmatic gate). Cosmetic → prompt.\n\nEXAMPLE: Refunds require a verified customer. Put a PreToolUse gate on process_refund that denies until get_customer verified the ID.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Stronger wording ('ALWAYS', caps, repeated) is still probabilistic; it fails sometimes, and you can't prove it won't.","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":13,"originalText":"DECIDING QUESTION: Must this ALWAYS happen (or never), or is it a preference?\n\nLOOK FOR → HOOK: 'must always', 'compliance', 'regulated', 'financial', 'audit', 'cannot be skipped', 'after a model upgrade it stopped'. LOOK FOR → PROMPT:\n'tone', 'style', 'preferred', 'persona'.\nIf breaking the rule is a P0 incident, it goes in code.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Ask what happens if Claude ignores the rule once. Serious/irreversible → hook (or other programmatic gate). Cosmetic → prompt.\n\nEXAMPLE: Refunds require a verified customer. Put a PreToolUse gate on process_refund that denies until get_customer verified the ID.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Stronger wording ('ALWAYS', caps, repeated) is still probabilistic; it fails sometimes, and you can't prove it won't.","height":188.76000000000002,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":288,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":16,"angle":0,"y":4382.72,"width":1118},{"isDeleted":false,"locked":false,"strokeColor":"#b4303a","link":null,"roughness":1,"groupIds":[],"backgroundColor":"#fff0f0","angle":0,"fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"version":1,"roundness":{"type":3},"type":"rectangle","updated":1791909281572,"id":"r-289","x":1190,"seed":290,"boundElements":null,"y":4339.72,"opacity":100,"height":251.76000000000002,"width":1150,"frameId":null,"strokeStyle":"solid","versionNonce":291},{"seed":293,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-292","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"2 · PreToolUse  vs  PostToolUse","fontSize":17,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"2 · PreToolUse  vs  PostToolUse","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":17,"originalText":"2 · PreToolUse  vs  PostToolUse","height":23,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":294,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":1206,"angle":0,"y":4351.72,"width":1118},{"seed":296,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-295","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"DECIDING QUESTION: Are you controlling what GOES TO a tool, or what COMES BACK from it?\n\nLOOK FOR → PRE: 'block', 'deny', 'before it runs', 'rewrite the arguments', 'policy', 'redirect to another tool'. LOOK FOR → POST: 'normalize', 'convert\nformats', 'before the model sees the result', 'log after success'.\nPre can stop a call. Post cannot — the call already happened.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Is the goal to PREVENT an action → Pre. Is the goal to CLEAN or RECORD a result → Post.\n\nEXAMPLE: Three servers return dates in different formats → PostToolUse converts them. A refund over $500 → PreToolUse denies.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Using Post to 'block' a bad refund is too late; using Pre to fix result formats can't work because there is no result yet.","fontSize":13,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"DECIDING QUESTION: Are you controlling what GOES TO a tool, or what COMES BACK from it?\n\nLOOK FOR → PRE: 'block', 'deny', 'before it runs', 'rewrite the arguments', 'policy', 'redirect to another tool'. LOOK FOR → POST: 'normalize', 'convert\nformats', 'before the model sees the result', 'log after success'.\nPre can stop a call. Post cannot — the call already happened.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Is the goal to PREVENT an action → Pre. Is the goal to CLEAN or RECORD a result → Post.\n\nEXAMPLE: Three servers return dates in different formats → PostToolUse converts them. A refund over $500 → PreToolUse denies.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Using Post to 'block' a bad refund is too late; using Pre to fix result formats can't work because there is no result yet.","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":13,"originalText":"DECIDING QUESTION: Are you controlling what GOES TO a tool, or what COMES BACK from it?\n\nLOOK FOR → PRE: 'block', 'deny', 'before it runs', 'rewrite the arguments', 'policy', 'redirect to another tool'. LOOK FOR → POST: 'normalize', 'convert\nformats', 'before the model sees the result', 'log after success'.\nPre can stop a call. Post cannot — the call already happened.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Is the goal to PREVENT an action → Pre. Is the goal to CLEAN or RECORD a result → Post.\n\nEXAMPLE: Three servers return dates in different formats → PostToolUse converts them. A refund over $500 → PreToolUse denies.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Using Post to 'block' a bad refund is too late; using Pre to fix result formats can't work because there is no result yet.","height":188.76000000000002,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":297,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":1206,"angle":0,"y":4382.72,"width":1118},{"isDeleted":false,"locked":false,"strokeColor":"#b4303a","link":null,"roughness":1,"groupIds":[],"backgroundColor":"#fff0f0","angle":0,"fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"version":1,"roundness":{"type":3},"type":"rectangle","updated":1791909281572,"id":"r-298","x":0,"seed":299,"boundElements":null,"y":4615.4800000000005,"opacity":100,"height":251.76000000000002,"width":1150,"frameId":null,"strokeStyle":"solid","versionNonce":300},{"seed":302,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-301","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"3 · updatedToolOutput  vs  additionalContext  vs  updatedInput","fontSize":17,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"3 · updatedToolOutput  vs  additionalContext  vs  updatedInput","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":17,"originalText":"3 · updatedToolOutput  vs  additionalContext  vs  updatedInput","height":23,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":303,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":16,"angle":0,"y":4627.4800000000005,"width":1118},{"seed":305,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-304","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"DECIDING QUESTION: Replace, append, or rewrite the INPUT?\n\nLOOK FOR → updatedToolOutput: 'replace what the model sees', 'convert/normalize'. LOOK FOR → additionalContext: 'add a note/reminder', 'append'.\nLOOK FOR → updatedInput: 'modify the tool's arguments' (PreToolUse) — only applies when the same hook returns permissionDecision 'allow'.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Output replaced → updatedToolOutput. Output kept but extended → additionalContext. Arguments changed → updatedInput + allow.\n\nEXAMPLE: A hook must hide an internal field from results → updatedToolOutput. A hook adds 'this data may be stale' → additionalContext.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Using additionalContext when the original bad data must disappear leaves it visible; forgetting allow with updatedInput means the\nrewrite is ignored.","fontSize":13,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"DECIDING QUESTION: Replace, append, or rewrite the INPUT?\n\nLOOK FOR → updatedToolOutput: 'replace what the model sees', 'convert/normalize'. LOOK FOR → additionalContext: 'add a note/reminder', 'append'.\nLOOK FOR → updatedInput: 'modify the tool's arguments' (PreToolUse) — only applies when the same hook returns permissionDecision 'allow'.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Output replaced → updatedToolOutput. Output kept but extended → additionalContext. Arguments changed → updatedInput + allow.\n\nEXAMPLE: A hook must hide an internal field from results → updatedToolOutput. A hook adds 'this data may be stale' → additionalContext.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Using additionalContext when the original bad data must disappear leaves it visible; forgetting allow with updatedInput means the\nrewrite is ignored.","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":13,"originalText":"DECIDING QUESTION: Replace, append, or rewrite the INPUT?\n\nLOOK FOR → updatedToolOutput: 'replace what the model sees', 'convert/normalize'. LOOK FOR → additionalContext: 'add a note/reminder', 'append'.\nLOOK FOR → updatedInput: 'modify the tool's arguments' (PreToolUse) — only applies when the same hook returns permissionDecision 'allow'.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Output replaced → updatedToolOutput. Output kept but extended → additionalContext. Arguments changed → updatedInput + allow.\n\nEXAMPLE: A hook must hide an internal field from results → updatedToolOutput. A hook adds 'this data may be stale' → additionalContext.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Using additionalContext when the original bad data must disappear leaves it visible; forgetting allow with updatedInput means the\nrewrite is ignored.","height":188.76000000000002,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":306,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":16,"angle":0,"y":4658.4800000000005,"width":1118},{"isDeleted":false,"locked":false,"strokeColor":"#b4303a","link":null,"roughness":1,"groupIds":[],"backgroundColor":"#fff0f0","angle":0,"fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"version":1,"roundness":{"type":3},"type":"rectangle","updated":1791909281572,"id":"r-307","x":1190,"seed":308,"boundElements":null,"y":4615.4800000000005,"opacity":100,"height":286.08000000000004,"width":1150,"frameId":null,"strokeStyle":"solid","versionNonce":309},{"seed":311,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-310","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"4 · PROMPT CHAINING  vs  ORCHESTRATOR-WORKERS (ADAPTIVE)","fontSize":17,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"4 · PROMPT CHAINING  vs  ORCHESTRATOR-WORKERS (ADAPTIVE)","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":17,"originalText":"4 · PROMPT CHAINING  vs  ORCHESTRATOR-WORKERS (ADAPTIVE)","height":23,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":312,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":1206,"angle":0,"y":4627.4800000000005,"width":1118},{"seed":314,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-313","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"DECIDING QUESTION: Are the steps known in advance, or discovered while working?\n\nLOOK FOR → CHAINING: 'same steps for every input', 'known sequence', 'each step feeds the next', 'gates between steps'. LOOK FOR → ADAPTIVE: 'depends on\nwhat is found', 'open-ended', 'number of subtasks unknown'.\nChaining is simpler and more predictable; adaptive costs more.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Could you write the full step list before seeing the input? Yes → chain. No → adaptive.\n\nEXAMPLE: Extract → validate → summarize contracts is a chain. 'Add tests to a legacy codebase' is adaptive: it finds modules, builds a backlog, and updates\nit.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Forcing an adaptive task into a fixed chain misses discovered work; using orchestrator-workers on a fixed task adds cost and failure\npoints.","fontSize":13,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"DECIDING QUESTION: Are the steps known in advance, or discovered while working?\n\nLOOK FOR → CHAINING: 'same steps for every input', 'known sequence', 'each step feeds the next', 'gates between steps'. LOOK FOR → ADAPTIVE: 'depends on\nwhat is found', 'open-ended', 'number of subtasks unknown'.\nChaining is simpler and more predictable; adaptive costs more.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Could you write the full step list before seeing the input? Yes → chain. No → adaptive.\n\nEXAMPLE: Extract → validate → summarize contracts is a chain. 'Add tests to a legacy codebase' is adaptive: it finds modules, builds a backlog, and updates\nit.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Forcing an adaptive task into a fixed chain misses discovered work; using orchestrator-workers on a fixed task adds cost and failure\npoints.","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":13,"originalText":"DECIDING QUESTION: Are the steps known in advance, or discovered while working?\n\nLOOK FOR → CHAINING: 'same steps for every input', 'known sequence', 'each step feeds the next', 'gates between steps'. LOOK FOR → ADAPTIVE: 'depends on\nwhat is found', 'open-ended', 'number of subtasks unknown'.\nChaining is simpler and more predictable; adaptive costs more.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Could you write the full step list before seeing the input? Yes → chain. No → adaptive.\n\nEXAMPLE: Extract → validate → summarize contracts is a chain. 'Add tests to a legacy codebase' is adaptive: it finds modules, builds a backlog, and updates\nit.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Forcing an adaptive task into a fixed chain misses discovered work; using orchestrator-workers on a fixed task adds cost and failure\npoints.","height":223.08,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":315,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":1206,"angle":0,"y":4658.4800000000005,"width":1118},{"isDeleted":false,"locked":false,"strokeColor":"#b4303a","link":null,"roughness":1,"groupIds":[],"backgroundColor":"#fff0f0","angle":0,"fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"version":1,"roundness":{"type":3},"type":"rectangle","updated":1791909281572,"id":"r-316","x":0,"seed":317,"boundElements":null,"y":4925.56,"opacity":100,"height":234.6,"width":1150,"frameId":null,"strokeStyle":"solid","versionNonce":318},{"seed":320,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-319","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"5 · PER-FILE + CROSS-FILE PASS  vs  ONE BIG PASS","fontSize":17,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"5 · PER-FILE + CROSS-FILE PASS  vs  ONE BIG PASS","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":17,"originalText":"5 · PER-FILE + CROSS-FILE PASS  vs  ONE BIG PASS","height":23,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":321,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":16,"angle":0,"y":4937.56,"width":1118},{"seed":323,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-322","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"DECIDING QUESTION: Is the input too large for one focused analysis?\n\nLOOK FOR → TWO PASSES: 'large pull request', 'many files', 'misses issues', 'inconsistent depth', 'attention'. LOOK FOR → ONE PASS: small change, few files.\nPer-file catches local problems; the cross-file pass catches interaction problems.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: If the input is large, split per item (parallel), then run ONE integration pass over the results. If small, one pass is fine.\n\nEXAMPLE: A 40-file PR: review each file separately with a baseline fork, then one call looks for API drift and type mismatches across files.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: One giant pass dilutes attention. Per-file only misses cross-file bugs. A bigger context window doesn't fix attention dilution.","fontSize":13,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"DECIDING QUESTION: Is the input too large for one focused analysis?\n\nLOOK FOR → TWO PASSES: 'large pull request', 'many files', 'misses issues', 'inconsistent depth', 'attention'. LOOK FOR → ONE PASS: small change, few files.\nPer-file catches local problems; the cross-file pass catches interaction problems.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: If the input is large, split per item (parallel), then run ONE integration pass over the results. If small, one pass is fine.\n\nEXAMPLE: A 40-file PR: review each file separately with a baseline fork, then one call looks for API drift and type mismatches across files.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: One giant pass dilutes attention. Per-file only misses cross-file bugs. A bigger context window doesn't fix attention dilution.","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":13,"originalText":"DECIDING QUESTION: Is the input too large for one focused analysis?\n\nLOOK FOR → TWO PASSES: 'large pull request', 'many files', 'misses issues', 'inconsistent depth', 'attention'. LOOK FOR → ONE PASS: small change, few files.\nPer-file catches local problems; the cross-file pass catches interaction problems.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: If the input is large, split per item (parallel), then run ONE integration pass over the results. If small, one pass is fine.\n\nEXAMPLE: A 40-file PR: review each file separately with a baseline fork, then one call looks for API drift and type mismatches across files.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: One giant pass dilutes attention. Per-file only misses cross-file bugs. A bigger context window doesn't fix attention dilution.","height":171.6,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":324,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":16,"angle":0,"y":4968.56,"width":1118},{"isDeleted":false,"locked":false,"strokeColor":"#b4303a","link":null,"roughness":1,"groupIds":[],"backgroundColor":"#fff0f0","angle":0,"fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"version":1,"roundness":{"type":3},"type":"rectangle","updated":1791909281572,"id":"r-325","x":1190,"seed":326,"boundElements":null,"y":4925.56,"opacity":100,"height":268.92,"width":1150,"frameId":null,"strokeStyle":"solid","versionNonce":327},{"seed":329,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-328","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"6 · RESUME  vs  CONTINUE  vs  FORK  vs  FRESH WITH SUMMARY","fontSize":17,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"6 · RESUME  vs  CONTINUE  vs  FORK  vs  FRESH WITH SUMMARY","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":17,"originalText":"6 · RESUME  vs  CONTINUE  vs  FORK  vs  FRESH WITH SUMMARY","height":23,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":330,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":1206,"angle":0,"y":4937.56,"width":1118},{"seed":332,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-331","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"DECIDING QUESTION: What do you need from the old conversation?\n\nLOOK FOR → CONTINUE: 'most recent session in this directory'. LOOK FOR → RESUME: 'a specific session by ID or name'. LOOK FOR → FORK: 'compare approaches\nfrom the same baseline'. LOOK FOR → FRESH: 'files changed a lot', 'stale', 'hallucinating old functions'.\nFork needs a session to branch from (resume/continue).\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Same conversation, keep going → resume/continue. Branch off to compare → fork. Context out of date and broad changes → new session + written\nsummary.\n\nEXAMPLE: Two refactor strategies from one analysis → fork twice. Repo refactored overnight → start fresh with goal, decisions, code state, open questions.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Resuming a stale session replays outdated tool results; forking doesn't refresh them either.","fontSize":13,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"DECIDING QUESTION: What do you need from the old conversation?\n\nLOOK FOR → CONTINUE: 'most recent session in this directory'. LOOK FOR → RESUME: 'a specific session by ID or name'. LOOK FOR → FORK: 'compare approaches\nfrom the same baseline'. LOOK FOR → FRESH: 'files changed a lot', 'stale', 'hallucinating old functions'.\nFork needs a session to branch from (resume/continue).\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Same conversation, keep going → resume/continue. Branch off to compare → fork. Context out of date and broad changes → new session + written\nsummary.\n\nEXAMPLE: Two refactor strategies from one analysis → fork twice. Repo refactored overnight → start fresh with goal, decisions, code state, open questions.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Resuming a stale session replays outdated tool results; forking doesn't refresh them either.","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":13,"originalText":"DECIDING QUESTION: What do you need from the old conversation?\n\nLOOK FOR → CONTINUE: 'most recent session in this directory'. LOOK FOR → RESUME: 'a specific session by ID or name'. LOOK FOR → FORK: 'compare approaches\nfrom the same baseline'. LOOK FOR → FRESH: 'files changed a lot', 'stale', 'hallucinating old functions'.\nFork needs a session to branch from (resume/continue).\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Same conversation, keep going → resume/continue. Branch off to compare → fork. Context out of date and broad changes → new session + written\nsummary.\n\nEXAMPLE: Two refactor strategies from one analysis → fork twice. Repo refactored overnight → start fresh with goal, decisions, code state, open questions.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Resuming a stale session replays outdated tool results; forking doesn't refresh them either.","height":205.92000000000002,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":333,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":1206,"angle":0,"y":4968.56,"width":1118},{"isDeleted":false,"locked":false,"strokeColor":"#b4303a","link":null,"roughness":1,"groupIds":[],"backgroundColor":"#fff0f0","angle":0,"fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"version":1,"roundness":{"type":3},"type":"rectangle","updated":1791909281572,"id":"r-334","x":0,"seed":335,"boundElements":null,"y":5218.4800000000005,"opacity":100,"height":251.76000000000002,"width":1150,"frameId":null,"strokeStyle":"solid","versionNonce":336},{"seed":338,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-337","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"7 · FORK_SESSION  vs  PARALLEL SUBAGENT DECOMPOSITION","fontSize":17,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"7 · FORK_SESSION  vs  PARALLEL SUBAGENT DECOMPOSITION","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":17,"originalText":"7 · FORK_SESSION  vs  PARALLEL SUBAGENT DECOMPOSITION","height":23,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":339,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":16,"angle":0,"y":5230.4800000000005,"width":1118},{"seed":341,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-340","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"DECIDING QUESTION: Same starting point, different directions — or different subtasks?\n\nLOOK FOR → FORK: 'try two approaches', 'baseline', 'A/B', 'risky exploration'. LOOK FOR → SUBAGENTS: 'split into independent pieces', 'research different\ntopics in parallel'.\nForks share history up to the fork point; subagents start with only what you pass.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Do all branches need the SAME loaded context and attempt alternatives? Fork. Are the pieces DIFFERENT jobs? Subagents (multiple Task calls).\n\nEXAMPLE: Compare a SQL rewrite vs adding an index → fork. Research 4 different industries → 4 subagents.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Using fork for different subtasks drags irrelevant shared history along; using subagents for A/B loses the shared baseline.","fontSize":13,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"DECIDING QUESTION: Same starting point, different directions — or different subtasks?\n\nLOOK FOR → FORK: 'try two approaches', 'baseline', 'A/B', 'risky exploration'. LOOK FOR → SUBAGENTS: 'split into independent pieces', 'research different\ntopics in parallel'.\nForks share history up to the fork point; subagents start with only what you pass.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Do all branches need the SAME loaded context and attempt alternatives? Fork. Are the pieces DIFFERENT jobs? Subagents (multiple Task calls).\n\nEXAMPLE: Compare a SQL rewrite vs adding an index → fork. Research 4 different industries → 4 subagents.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Using fork for different subtasks drags irrelevant shared history along; using subagents for A/B loses the shared baseline.","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":13,"originalText":"DECIDING QUESTION: Same starting point, different directions — or different subtasks?\n\nLOOK FOR → FORK: 'try two approaches', 'baseline', 'A/B', 'risky exploration'. LOOK FOR → SUBAGENTS: 'split into independent pieces', 'research different\ntopics in parallel'.\nForks share history up to the fork point; subagents start with only what you pass.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Do all branches need the SAME loaded context and attempt alternatives? Fork. Are the pieces DIFFERENT jobs? Subagents (multiple Task calls).\n\nEXAMPLE: Compare a SQL rewrite vs adding an index → fork. Research 4 different industries → 4 subagents.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Using fork for different subtasks drags irrelevant shared history along; using subagents for A/B loses the shared baseline.","height":188.76000000000002,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":342,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":16,"angle":0,"y":5261.4800000000005,"width":1118},{"isDeleted":false,"locked":false,"strokeColor":"#b4303a","link":null,"roughness":1,"groupIds":[],"backgroundColor":"#fff0f0","angle":0,"fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"version":1,"roundness":{"type":3},"type":"rectangle","updated":1791909281572,"id":"r-343","x":1190,"seed":344,"boundElements":null,"y":5218.4800000000005,"opacity":100,"height":234.6,"width":1150,"frameId":null,"strokeStyle":"solid","versionNonce":345},{"seed":347,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-346","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"8 · Task IN COORDINATOR allowedTools  vs  Task IN SUBAGENT tools","fontSize":17,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"8 · Task IN COORDINATOR allowedTools  vs  Task IN SUBAGENT tools","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":17,"originalText":"8 · Task IN COORDINATOR allowedTools  vs  Task IN SUBAGENT tools","height":23,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":348,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":1206,"angle":0,"y":5230.4800000000005,"width":1118},{"seed":350,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-349","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"DECIDING QUESTION: Who is allowed to spawn subagents?\n\nLOOK FOR → COORDINATOR: 'nothing gets spawned', 'delegation fails'. LOOK FOR → SUBAGENT: 'subagents spawning subagents', 'runaway recursion'.\nOnly the coordinator spawns.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Spawning not working → add Task (Agent) to the coordinator's allowedTools. Never add it to a subagent's tools.\n\nEXAMPLE: Coordinator defines web-researcher and doc-analyst with narrow tools; Task sits in the coordinator's allowedTools only.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Giving subagents Task enables uncontrolled recursion and breaks hub-and-spoke observability.","fontSize":13,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"DECIDING QUESTION: Who is allowed to spawn subagents?\n\nLOOK FOR → COORDINATOR: 'nothing gets spawned', 'delegation fails'. LOOK FOR → SUBAGENT: 'subagents spawning subagents', 'runaway recursion'.\nOnly the coordinator spawns.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Spawning not working → add Task (Agent) to the coordinator's allowedTools. Never add it to a subagent's tools.\n\nEXAMPLE: Coordinator defines web-researcher and doc-analyst with narrow tools; Task sits in the coordinator's allowedTools only.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Giving subagents Task enables uncontrolled recursion and breaks hub-and-spoke observability.","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":13,"originalText":"DECIDING QUESTION: Who is allowed to spawn subagents?\n\nLOOK FOR → COORDINATOR: 'nothing gets spawned', 'delegation fails'. LOOK FOR → SUBAGENT: 'subagents spawning subagents', 'runaway recursion'.\nOnly the coordinator spawns.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Spawning not working → add Task (Agent) to the coordinator's allowedTools. Never add it to a subagent's tools.\n\nEXAMPLE: Coordinator defines web-researcher and doc-analyst with narrow tools; Task sits in the coordinator's allowedTools only.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Giving subagents Task enables uncontrolled recursion and breaks hub-and-spoke observability.","height":171.6,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":351,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":1206,"angle":0,"y":5261.4800000000005,"width":1118},{"isDeleted":false,"locked":false,"strokeColor":"#b4303a","link":null,"roughness":1,"groupIds":[],"backgroundColor":"#fff0f0","angle":0,"fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"version":1,"roundness":{"type":3},"type":"rectangle","updated":1791909281572,"id":"r-352","x":0,"seed":353,"boundElements":null,"y":5494.240000000001,"opacity":100,"height":251.76000000000002,"width":1150,"frameId":null,"strokeStyle":"solid","versionNonce":354},{"seed":356,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-355","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"9 · MULTIPLE Task CALLS IN ONE TURN  vs  ONE PER TURN","fontSize":17,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"9 · MULTIPLE Task CALLS IN ONE TURN  vs  ONE PER TURN","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":17,"originalText":"9 · MULTIPLE Task CALLS IN ONE TURN  vs  ONE PER TURN","height":23,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":357,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":16,"angle":0,"y":5506.240000000001,"width":1118},{"seed":359,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-358","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"DECIDING QUESTION: Is research slow because subagents run one after another?\n\nLOOK FOR → ONE TURN: 'independent subagents', 'latency grows with each subagent'. LOOK FOR → SEPARATE TURNS: only when a later subagent needs an earlier\none's result.\nParallel = several tool calls in a SINGLE assistant response.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Independent work → instruct the coordinator to emit all Task calls in one response. Dependent work → sequential.\n\nEXAMPLE: Four independent topic researchers launched in one turn finish in the time of the slowest, not the sum of all four.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: 'Run them one at a time to keep it safe' only adds latency (same as Part A's parallel-vs-sequential rule).","fontSize":13,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"DECIDING QUESTION: Is research slow because subagents run one after another?\n\nLOOK FOR → ONE TURN: 'independent subagents', 'latency grows with each subagent'. LOOK FOR → SEPARATE TURNS: only when a later subagent needs an earlier\none's result.\nParallel = several tool calls in a SINGLE assistant response.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Independent work → instruct the coordinator to emit all Task calls in one response. Dependent work → sequential.\n\nEXAMPLE: Four independent topic researchers launched in one turn finish in the time of the slowest, not the sum of all four.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: 'Run them one at a time to keep it safe' only adds latency (same as Part A's parallel-vs-sequential rule).","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":13,"originalText":"DECIDING QUESTION: Is research slow because subagents run one after another?\n\nLOOK FOR → ONE TURN: 'independent subagents', 'latency grows with each subagent'. LOOK FOR → SEPARATE TURNS: only when a later subagent needs an earlier\none's result.\nParallel = several tool calls in a SINGLE assistant response.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Independent work → instruct the coordinator to emit all Task calls in one response. Dependent work → sequential.\n\nEXAMPLE: Four independent topic researchers launched in one turn finish in the time of the slowest, not the sum of all four.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: 'Run them one at a time to keep it safe' only adds latency (same as Part A's parallel-vs-sequential rule).","height":188.76000000000002,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":360,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":16,"angle":0,"y":5537.240000000001,"width":1118},{"isDeleted":false,"locked":false,"strokeColor":"#b4303a","link":null,"roughness":1,"groupIds":[],"backgroundColor":"#fff0f0","angle":0,"fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"version":1,"roundness":{"type":3},"type":"rectangle","updated":1791909281572,"id":"r-361","x":1190,"seed":362,"boundElements":null,"y":5494.240000000001,"opacity":100,"height":251.76000000000002,"width":1150,"frameId":null,"strokeStyle":"solid","versionNonce":363},{"seed":365,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-364","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"10 · SUBAGENT 'REMEMBERS' CONTEXT  vs  EXPLICIT BRIEFING","fontSize":17,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"10 · SUBAGENT 'REMEMBERS' CONTEXT  vs  EXPLICIT BRIEFING","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":17,"originalText":"10 · SUBAGENT 'REMEMBERS' CONTEXT  vs  EXPLICIT BRIEFING","height":23,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":366,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":1206,"angle":0,"y":5506.240000000001,"width":1118},{"seed":368,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-367","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"DECIDING QUESTION: Does the subagent inherit the parent's conversation?\n\nLOOK FOR → EXPLICIT: 'invents URLs', 'repeats searches', 'contradicts earlier findings', 'lost citations'. LOOK FOR → INHERITED (wrong belief): 'it should\nalready know'.\nSubagents inherit NOTHING beyond their definition prompt + the Task string + tools.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Check the Task prompt. If prior findings, paths, constraints or metadata aren't in it, the subagent doesn't have them.\n\nEXAMPLE: Pass prior_findings with source_url, page and retrieved_at in structured JSON plus open_questions and 'do not' lines.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Assuming shared memory causes context starvation; dumping the whole parent transcript breaks isolation and wastes tokens.","fontSize":13,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"DECIDING QUESTION: Does the subagent inherit the parent's conversation?\n\nLOOK FOR → EXPLICIT: 'invents URLs', 'repeats searches', 'contradicts earlier findings', 'lost citations'. LOOK FOR → INHERITED (wrong belief): 'it should\nalready know'.\nSubagents inherit NOTHING beyond their definition prompt + the Task string + tools.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Check the Task prompt. If prior findings, paths, constraints or metadata aren't in it, the subagent doesn't have them.\n\nEXAMPLE: Pass prior_findings with source_url, page and retrieved_at in structured JSON plus open_questions and 'do not' lines.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Assuming shared memory causes context starvation; dumping the whole parent transcript breaks isolation and wastes tokens.","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":13,"originalText":"DECIDING QUESTION: Does the subagent inherit the parent's conversation?\n\nLOOK FOR → EXPLICIT: 'invents URLs', 'repeats searches', 'contradicts earlier findings', 'lost citations'. LOOK FOR → INHERITED (wrong belief): 'it should\nalready know'.\nSubagents inherit NOTHING beyond their definition prompt + the Task string + tools.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Check the Task prompt. If prior findings, paths, constraints or metadata aren't in it, the subagent doesn't have them.\n\nEXAMPLE: Pass prior_findings with source_url, page and retrieved_at in structured JSON plus open_questions and 'do not' lines.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Assuming shared memory causes context starvation; dumping the whole parent transcript breaks isolation and wastes tokens.","height":188.76000000000002,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":369,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":1206,"angle":0,"y":5537.240000000001,"width":1118},{"isDeleted":false,"locked":false,"strokeColor":"#b4303a","link":null,"roughness":1,"groupIds":[],"backgroundColor":"#fff0f0","angle":0,"fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"version":1,"roundness":{"type":3},"type":"rectangle","updated":1791909281572,"id":"r-370","x":0,"seed":371,"boundElements":null,"y":5770.000000000001,"opacity":100,"height":268.92,"width":1150,"frameId":null,"strokeStyle":"solid","versionNonce":372},{"seed":374,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-373","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"11 · COORDINATOR DECOMPOSITION FAULT  vs  SUBAGENT FAULT","fontSize":17,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"11 · COORDINATOR DECOMPOSITION FAULT  vs  SUBAGENT FAULT","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":17,"originalText":"11 · COORDINATOR DECOMPOSITION FAULT  vs  SUBAGENT FAULT","height":23,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":375,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":16,"angle":0,"y":5782.000000000001,"width":1118},{"seed":377,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-376","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"DECIDING QUESTION: Did the parts fail, or were the wrong parts chosen?\n\nLOOK FOR → COORDINATOR: 'every subagent succeeded but topics are missing', 'incomplete coverage'. LOOK FOR → SUBAGENT: 'one subagent returned bad/low-\nquality output'.\nRoot cause lives where the decision was made.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: If all workers did their assigned job correctly yet the whole is incomplete → the split was wrong. Fix breadth check + gap audit + re-\ndelegate.\n\nEXAMPLE: 'Creative industries' split into 3 visual arts topics; music, writing, film never assigned.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Blaming subagents or buying a bigger model won't add topics nobody was asked to research.","fontSize":13,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"DECIDING QUESTION: Did the parts fail, or were the wrong parts chosen?\n\nLOOK FOR → COORDINATOR: 'every subagent succeeded but topics are missing', 'incomplete coverage'. LOOK FOR → SUBAGENT: 'one subagent returned bad/low-\nquality output'.\nRoot cause lives where the decision was made.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: If all workers did their assigned job correctly yet the whole is incomplete → the split was wrong. Fix breadth check + gap audit + re-\ndelegate.\n\nEXAMPLE: 'Creative industries' split into 3 visual arts topics; music, writing, film never assigned.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Blaming subagents or buying a bigger model won't add topics nobody was asked to research.","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":13,"originalText":"DECIDING QUESTION: Did the parts fail, or were the wrong parts chosen?\n\nLOOK FOR → COORDINATOR: 'every subagent succeeded but topics are missing', 'incomplete coverage'. LOOK FOR → SUBAGENT: 'one subagent returned bad/low-\nquality output'.\nRoot cause lives where the decision was made.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: If all workers did their assigned job correctly yet the whole is incomplete → the split was wrong. Fix breadth check + gap audit + re-\ndelegate.\n\nEXAMPLE: 'Creative industries' split into 3 visual arts topics; music, writing, film never assigned.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Blaming subagents or buying a bigger model won't add topics nobody was asked to research.","height":205.92000000000002,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":378,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":16,"angle":0,"y":5813.000000000001,"width":1118},{"isDeleted":false,"locked":false,"strokeColor":"#b4303a","link":null,"roughness":1,"groupIds":[],"backgroundColor":"#fff0f0","angle":0,"fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"version":1,"roundness":{"type":3},"type":"rectangle","updated":1791909281572,"id":"r-379","x":1190,"seed":380,"boundElements":null,"y":5770.000000000001,"opacity":100,"height":251.76000000000002,"width":1150,"frameId":null,"strokeStyle":"solid","versionNonce":381},{"seed":383,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-382","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"12 · end_turn  vs  max_tokens  vs  pause_turn  vs  refusal","fontSize":17,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"12 · end_turn  vs  max_tokens  vs  pause_turn  vs  refusal","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":17,"originalText":"12 · end_turn  vs  max_tokens  vs  pause_turn  vs  refusal","height":23,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":384,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":1206,"angle":0,"y":5782.000000000001,"width":1118},{"seed":386,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-385","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"DECIDING QUESTION: What did the stop_reason say happened?\n\nLOOK FOR → end_turn: finished. → max_tokens: output cut off (maybe mid tool_use). → pause_turn: server tool loop paused. → refusal: safety decline.\nEach needs a different loop action.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: end_turn: exit. max_tokens: raise limit/continue. pause_turn: append unchanged and call again. refusal: surface/escalate, don't loop.\n\nEXAMPLE: Response ends in a half-written tool_use → retry with higher max_tokens, not the same one.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Treating pause_turn as done truncates work; looping on refusal repeats a decline; retrying max_tokens with the same value fails\nagain.","fontSize":13,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"DECIDING QUESTION: What did the stop_reason say happened?\n\nLOOK FOR → end_turn: finished. → max_tokens: output cut off (maybe mid tool_use). → pause_turn: server tool loop paused. → refusal: safety decline.\nEach needs a different loop action.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: end_turn: exit. max_tokens: raise limit/continue. pause_turn: append unchanged and call again. refusal: surface/escalate, don't loop.\n\nEXAMPLE: Response ends in a half-written tool_use → retry with higher max_tokens, not the same one.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Treating pause_turn as done truncates work; looping on refusal repeats a decline; retrying max_tokens with the same value fails\nagain.","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":13,"originalText":"DECIDING QUESTION: What did the stop_reason say happened?\n\nLOOK FOR → end_turn: finished. → max_tokens: output cut off (maybe mid tool_use). → pause_turn: server tool loop paused. → refusal: safety decline.\nEach needs a different loop action.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: end_turn: exit. max_tokens: raise limit/continue. pause_turn: append unchanged and call again. refusal: surface/escalate, don't loop.\n\nEXAMPLE: Response ends in a half-written tool_use → retry with higher max_tokens, not the same one.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Treating pause_turn as done truncates work; looping on refusal repeats a decline; retrying max_tokens with the same value fails\nagain.","height":188.76000000000002,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":387,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":1206,"angle":0,"y":5813.000000000001,"width":1118},{"isDeleted":false,"locked":false,"strokeColor":"#b4303a","link":null,"roughness":1,"groupIds":[],"backgroundColor":"#fff0f0","angle":0,"fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"version":1,"roundness":{"type":3},"type":"rectangle","updated":1791909281572,"id":"r-388","x":0,"seed":389,"boundElements":null,"y":6062.920000000001,"opacity":100,"height":234.6,"width":1150,"frameId":null,"strokeStyle":"solid","versionNonce":390},{"seed":392,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-391","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"13 · STOP ON stop_reason  vs  STOP ON AN ITERATION CAP / TEXT","fontSize":17,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"13 · STOP ON stop_reason  vs  STOP ON AN ITERATION CAP / TEXT","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":17,"originalText":"13 · STOP ON stop_reason  vs  STOP ON AN ITERATION CAP / TEXT","height":23,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":393,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":16,"angle":0,"y":6074.920000000001,"width":1118},{"seed":395,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-394","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"DECIDING QUESTION: What is the primary terminator?\n\nLOOK FOR → stop_reason: 'branch on stop_reason', 'no tool_use blocks'. LOOK FOR → WRONG: 'for _ in range(10)', 'if \"DONE\" in text'.\nCaps (max_turns, max_budget_usd) are guardrails only.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Primary stop = model signals end_turn (no tool_use). Keep caps as a safety net and handle their error subtypes.\n\nEXAMPLE: A loop with 'range(10)' truncates a hard task on turn 10 and wastes turns on easy ones.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Text-parsing is brittle and can quit while a tool call is pending; a cap as primary stop cuts off real work.","fontSize":13,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"DECIDING QUESTION: What is the primary terminator?\n\nLOOK FOR → stop_reason: 'branch on stop_reason', 'no tool_use blocks'. LOOK FOR → WRONG: 'for _ in range(10)', 'if \"DONE\" in text'.\nCaps (max_turns, max_budget_usd) are guardrails only.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Primary stop = model signals end_turn (no tool_use). Keep caps as a safety net and handle their error subtypes.\n\nEXAMPLE: A loop with 'range(10)' truncates a hard task on turn 10 and wastes turns on easy ones.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Text-parsing is brittle and can quit while a tool call is pending; a cap as primary stop cuts off real work.","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":13,"originalText":"DECIDING QUESTION: What is the primary terminator?\n\nLOOK FOR → stop_reason: 'branch on stop_reason', 'no tool_use blocks'. LOOK FOR → WRONG: 'for _ in range(10)', 'if \"DONE\" in text'.\nCaps (max_turns, max_budget_usd) are guardrails only.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Primary stop = model signals end_turn (no tool_use). Keep caps as a safety net and handle their error subtypes.\n\nEXAMPLE: A loop with 'range(10)' truncates a hard task on turn 10 and wastes turns on easy ones.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Text-parsing is brittle and can quit while a tool call is pending; a cap as primary stop cuts off real work.","height":171.6,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":396,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":16,"angle":0,"y":6105.920000000001,"width":1118},{"isDeleted":false,"locked":false,"strokeColor":"#b4303a","link":null,"roughness":1,"groupIds":[],"backgroundColor":"#fff0f0","angle":0,"fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"version":1,"roundness":{"type":3},"type":"rectangle","updated":1791909281572,"id":"r-397","x":1190,"seed":398,"boundElements":null,"y":6062.920000000001,"opacity":100,"height":251.76000000000002,"width":1150,"frameId":null,"strokeStyle":"solid","versionNonce":399},{"seed":401,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-400","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"14 · STRUCTURED HANDOFF  vs  FORWARD THE TRANSCRIPT","fontSize":17,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"14 · STRUCTURED HANDOFF  vs  FORWARD THE TRANSCRIPT","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":17,"originalText":"14 · STRUCTURED HANDOFF  vs  FORWARD THE TRANSCRIPT","height":23,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":402,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":1206,"angle":0,"y":6074.920000000001,"width":1118},{"seed":404,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-403","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"DECIDING QUESTION: What does the human need to act without redoing work?\n\nLOOK FOR → STRUCTURED: 'human re-asks the customer', 'lack of context', 'escalation quality'. LOOK FOR → TRANSCRIPT: 'forward everything'.\nThe human often has no access to the transcript and no time to read it.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Provide customer ID + verification, case state, root cause + evidence, attempted actions, recommended action, policy refs — validated before\nsending.\n\nEXAMPLE: Refund > limit: handoff states verified customer, damaged item evidence, blocked by gate, and recommended manager approval.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: A raw transcript or 'customer is upset' forces re-verification and re-investigation.","fontSize":13,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"DECIDING QUESTION: What does the human need to act without redoing work?\n\nLOOK FOR → STRUCTURED: 'human re-asks the customer', 'lack of context', 'escalation quality'. LOOK FOR → TRANSCRIPT: 'forward everything'.\nThe human often has no access to the transcript and no time to read it.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Provide customer ID + verification, case state, root cause + evidence, attempted actions, recommended action, policy refs — validated before\nsending.\n\nEXAMPLE: Refund > limit: handoff states verified customer, damaged item evidence, blocked by gate, and recommended manager approval.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: A raw transcript or 'customer is upset' forces re-verification and re-investigation.","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":13,"originalText":"DECIDING QUESTION: What does the human need to act without redoing work?\n\nLOOK FOR → STRUCTURED: 'human re-asks the customer', 'lack of context', 'escalation quality'. LOOK FOR → TRANSCRIPT: 'forward everything'.\nThe human often has no access to the transcript and no time to read it.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Provide customer ID + verification, case state, root cause + evidence, attempted actions, recommended action, policy refs — validated before\nsending.\n\nEXAMPLE: Refund > limit: handoff states verified customer, damaged item evidence, blocked by gate, and recommended manager approval.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: A raw transcript or 'customer is upset' forces re-verification and re-investigation.","height":188.76000000000002,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":405,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":1206,"angle":0,"y":6105.920000000001,"width":1118},{"isDeleted":false,"locked":false,"strokeColor":"#b4303a","link":null,"roughness":1,"groupIds":[],"backgroundColor":"#fff0f0","angle":0,"fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"version":1,"roundness":{"type":3},"type":"rectangle","updated":1791909281572,"id":"r-406","x":0,"seed":407,"boundElements":null,"y":6338.680000000001,"opacity":100,"height":268.92,"width":1150,"frameId":null,"strokeStyle":"solid","versionNonce":408},{"seed":410,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-409","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"15 · SECTIONING  vs  VOTING  vs  ROUTING  vs  EVALUATOR-OPTIMIZER","fontSize":17,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"15 · SECTIONING  vs  VOTING  vs  ROUTING  vs  EVALUATOR-OPTIMIZER","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":17,"originalText":"15 · SECTIONING  vs  VOTING  vs  ROUTING  vs  EVALUATOR-OPTIMIZER","height":23,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":411,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":16,"angle":0,"y":6350.680000000001,"width":1118},{"seed":413,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-412","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"DECIDING QUESTION: What are the parallel/related pieces actually doing?\n\nLOOK FOR → SECTIONING: different independent parts. → VOTING: the SAME task repeated for confidence. → ROUTING: classify the input then send to a\nspecialist. → EVALUATOR-OPTIMIZER: generate, critique, improve loop.\nAll are workflow patterns the exam may name.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Different parts → sectioning. Same task, more confidence → voting. Distinct categories with specialists → routing. Quality improves with\ncritique → evaluator-optimizer.\n\nEXAMPLE: Classify support tickets then send billing to the billing specialist → routing.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Using voting where parts differ wastes calls; using routing where there's a single task adds a pointless classifier.","fontSize":13,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"DECIDING QUESTION: What are the parallel/related pieces actually doing?\n\nLOOK FOR → SECTIONING: different independent parts. → VOTING: the SAME task repeated for confidence. → ROUTING: classify the input then send to a\nspecialist. → EVALUATOR-OPTIMIZER: generate, critique, improve loop.\nAll are workflow patterns the exam may name.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Different parts → sectioning. Same task, more confidence → voting. Distinct categories with specialists → routing. Quality improves with\ncritique → evaluator-optimizer.\n\nEXAMPLE: Classify support tickets then send billing to the billing specialist → routing.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Using voting where parts differ wastes calls; using routing where there's a single task adds a pointless classifier.","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":13,"originalText":"DECIDING QUESTION: What are the parallel/related pieces actually doing?\n\nLOOK FOR → SECTIONING: different independent parts. → VOTING: the SAME task repeated for confidence. → ROUTING: classify the input then send to a\nspecialist. → EVALUATOR-OPTIMIZER: generate, critique, improve loop.\nAll are workflow patterns the exam may name.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Different parts → sectioning. Same task, more confidence → voting. Distinct categories with specialists → routing. Quality improves with\ncritique → evaluator-optimizer.\n\nEXAMPLE: Classify support tickets then send billing to the billing specialist → routing.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Using voting where parts differ wastes calls; using routing where there's a single task adds a pointless classifier.","height":205.92000000000002,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":414,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":16,"angle":0,"y":6381.680000000001,"width":1118},{"isDeleted":false,"locked":false,"strokeColor":"#b4303a","link":null,"roughness":1,"groupIds":[],"backgroundColor":"#fff0f0","angle":0,"fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":1,"version":1,"roundness":{"type":3},"type":"rectangle","updated":1791909281572,"id":"r-415","x":1190,"seed":416,"boundElements":null,"y":6338.680000000001,"opacity":100,"height":268.92,"width":1150,"frameId":null,"strokeStyle":"solid","versionNonce":417},{"seed":419,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-418","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"16 · OVER-PROVISIONED SUBAGENT  vs  SCOPED TOOL","fontSize":17,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"16 · OVER-PROVISIONED SUBAGENT  vs  SCOPED TOOL","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":17,"originalText":"16 · OVER-PROVISIONED SUBAGENT  vs  SCOPED TOOL","height":23,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#0f172a","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":420,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":1206,"angle":0,"y":6350.680000000001,"width":1118},{"seed":422,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-421","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"DECIDING QUESTION: Does a subagent need a whole toolset for a simple, frequent need?\n\nLOOK FOR → SCOPED TOOL: 'synthesis agent keeps going back to the coordinator for simple checks', 'most checks are simple'. LOOK FOR → FULL TOOLSET: rarely\ncorrect.\nLeast privilege wins.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Give the subagent a narrow tool (e.g., verify_fact) for the common ~85% case; route the complex remainder through the coordinator.\n\nEXAMPLE: A synthesis agent gets full web search and starts researching instead of synthesizing. Replace with a scoped verify_fact tool.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Full web access turns the agent into a researcher (scope creep, cost, risk); forcing every simple check through the coordinator adds\nlatency.","fontSize":13,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"DECIDING QUESTION: Does a subagent need a whole toolset for a simple, frequent need?\n\nLOOK FOR → SCOPED TOOL: 'synthesis agent keeps going back to the coordinator for simple checks', 'most checks are simple'. LOOK FOR → FULL TOOLSET: rarely\ncorrect.\nLeast privilege wins.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Give the subagent a narrow tool (e.g., verify_fact) for the common ~85% case; route the complex remainder through the coordinator.\n\nEXAMPLE: A synthesis agent gets full web search and starts researching instead of synthesizing. Replace with a scoped verify_fact tool.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Full web access turns the agent into a researcher (scope creep, cost, risk); forcing every simple check through the coordinator adds\nlatency.","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":13,"originalText":"DECIDING QUESTION: Does a subagent need a whole toolset for a simple, frequent need?\n\nLOOK FOR → SCOPED TOOL: 'synthesis agent keeps going back to the coordinator for simple checks', 'most checks are simple'. LOOK FOR → FULL TOOLSET: rarely\ncorrect.\nLeast privilege wins.\n\nHOW TO DECIDE: Give the subagent a narrow tool (e.g., verify_fact) for the common ~85% case; route the complex remainder through the coordinator.\n\nEXAMPLE: A synthesis agent gets full web search and starts researching instead of synthesizing. Replace with a scoped verify_fact tool.\n\nWHY THE TRAP IS WRONG: Full web access turns the agent into a researcher (scope creep, cost, risk); forcing every simple check through the coordinator adds\nlatency.","height":205.92000000000002,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#2b2b2b","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":423,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":1206,"angle":0,"y":6381.680000000001,"width":1118},{"seed":425,"strokeStyle":"solid","id":"t-424","strokeWidth":1,"roughness":1,"isDeleted":false,"rawText":"FAILURE / DECISION MATRIX","fontSize":26,"opacity":100,"verticalAlign":"top","updated":1791909281572,"locked":false,"text":"FAILURE / DECISION MATRIX","version":1,"type":"text","backgroundColor":"transparent","roundness":null,"containerId":null,"baseline":26,"originalText":"FAILURE / DECISION MATRIX","height":36,"link":null,"strokeColor":"#0f3d8a","fillStyle":"solid","groupIds":[],"versionNonce":426,"textAlign":"left","fontFamily":2,"lineHeight":1.25,"frameId":null,"boundElements":null,"x":0,"angle":0,"y":6651.600000000001,"width":2400},{"isDeleted":f